                                                                               skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                               skos:prefLabel "TXP16 employs script"@en ;
                                                                               skos:scopeNote "This property associates an instance of TX3 Writing System with one of the instances of the script (TX13) it employs."^^xsd:string .


###  http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TXP1_used_writing_system
<http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TXP1_used_writing_system> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                                                   rdfs:domain <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX2_Writing> ;
                                                                                   rdfs:range <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX3_Writing_System> ;
                                                                                   rdfs:comment "Inverse: writing system used for"@en ;
                                                                                   skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                                   skos:prefLabel "TXP1 used writing system"@en ;
                                                                                   skos:scopeNote "This property identifies the specific instance of TX3 Writing System employed during the writing event (TX2) that led to the creation of a written text (TX1) [epont:Inscribed Text]"^^xsd:string .


###  http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TXP4_has_segment
<http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TXP4_has_segment> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                                           rdfs:subPropertyOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P46> ;
                                                                           rdfs:domain epont:Inscribed_Text ;
                                                                           rdfs:range <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX7_Written_Text_Segment> ;
                                                                           rdfs:comment "Inverse: is segment of"@en ;
                                                                           skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                           skos:prefLabel "TXP4 has segment"@en ;
                                                                           skos:scopeNote "This property is intended to correlate a text and the different parts of it which a scholar can identify, such as: letters, words, lines, columns, pages, or any other scan that can be made by scholars because it is considered to have a particular relevance for the investigation of the text itself."^^xsd:string .


###  http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TXP5_wrote
<http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TXP5_wrote> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                                     rdfs:domain <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX2_Writing> ;
                                                                     rdfs:range epont:Inscribed_Text ;
                                                                     rdfs:comment "Inverse: was written by"@en ;
                                                                     skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                     skos:prefLabel "TXP5 wrote"@en ;
                                                                     skos:scopeNote "This property is used to describe in detail the close relationship between a text and the writing event that led to its production."^^xsd:string .


###  http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TXP6_encodes
<http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TXP6_encodes> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                                       rdfs:subPropertyOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P2> ;
                                                                       rdfs:domain <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX3_Writing_System> ;
                                                                       rdfs:range <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E56> ;
                                                                       rdfs:comment "Inverse: is encoding of"@en ;
                                                                       skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                       skos:prefLabel "TXP6 encodes"@en ;
                                                                       skos:scopeNote "This property is used to indicate the language encoded by the TX3 Writing System and used for writing, reading or rendering (i.e. transcribing) a TX1 Written Text."^^xsd:string .


###  http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TXP7_has_item
<http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TXP7_has_item> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                                        rdfs:domain <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX13_Script> ;
                                                                        rdfs:range epont:Ontograph ;
                                                                        rdfs:comment "Inverse: is item of"@en ;
                                                                        skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                        skos:note "This requires discussion and likely alteration, since the conceptual classification of both TX13 and TX8 seem unsatisfactory, and in turn this subproperty of 'P67 refers to' seems inappropriate."^^xsd:string ;
                                                                        skos:prefLabel "TXP7 has item"@en ;
                                                                        skos:scopeNote "This property associates an instance of TX13 Script with an instance of TX8 Grapheme employed by this script. Different instances of TX13 Script may have some graphemes in common."^^xsd:string .


###  http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TXP8_has_component
<http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TXP8_has_component> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                                             rdfs:subPropertyOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P46> ;
                                                                             rdfs:domain <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX7_Written_Text_Segment> ;
                                                                             rdfs:range epont:Graph ;
                                                                             rdfs:comment "Inverse: is component of"@en ;
                                                                             skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                             skos:prefLabel "TXP8 has component"@en ;
                                                                             skos:scopeNote "This property is used to state the (physical) belonging of a glyph[Graph] to a given TX1 Written Text [Inscribed Text]."^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org//html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P7
<https://cidoc-crm.org//html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P7> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                       rdfs:domain crm:E12_Production ,
                                                                   <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX2_Writing> ,
                                                                   <https://cidoc-crm.org//html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E63> ,
                                                                   <https://cidoc-crm.org//html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E64> ,
                                                                   epont:Transcribing ;
                                                       rdfs:range <https://cidoc-crm.org//html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E53> ;
                                                       rdfs:comment "Inverse: witnessed"@en ;
                                                       skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                       skos:prefLabel "P7 took place at"@en ;
                                                       skos:scopeNote """This property describes the spatial location of an instance of E4 Period.

The related instance of E53 Place should be seen as a wider approximation of the geometric area within which the phenomena that characterise the period in question occurred, see below. P7 took place at (witnessed) does not convey any meaning other than spatial positioning (frequently on the surface of the earth). For example, the period “Révolution française” can be said to have taken place in “France in 1789”; the “Victorian” period may be said to have taken place in “Britain from 1837-1901” and its colonies, as well as other parts of Europe and North America. An instance of E4 Period can take place at multiple non-contiguous, non-overlapping locations.

Any place where something happened includes the spatial projection of the happening given in the same geometric reference system. For instance, HMS Victory, as place of Lord Nelson's dying, includes the location of his body relative to the hull of HMS Victory at his time of death as the most precise location of his death. By the definition of P161 has spatial projection, an instance of E4 Period takes place on all its spatial projections to respective reference systems, that is, instances of E53 Place. Therefore, this property implies the more fully developed path from E4 Period through P161 has spatial projection, E53 Place, P89 falls within to E53 Place, where both places are defined in the same geometric reference system. The relation between an instance of E53 Place and its reference system can conveniently be documented via the property P157 is at rest relative to (provides reference space for).

Something that has happened at a given place can also be considered to have happened at a smaller place within it: for example, it is reasonable to say Caesar’s murder took place in Rome, but also on the Forum Romanum, and more precisely in the Curia. It is characteristic for different historical sources to use varying precision in such statements, without being in contradiction with each other. This may be due to lack of knowledge or to the relevance of the precision for the purpose of the statement. In information integration, the more precise statement improves the overall knowledge."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org//html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P92
<https://cidoc-crm.org//html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P92> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                        rdfs:domain <https://cidoc-crm.org//html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E63> ;
                                                        rdfs:range epont:Epigraphic_Support ,
                                                                   epont:Inscribed_Text ;
                                                        rdfs:comment "Inverse: was brought into existence by"@en ;
                                                        skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                        skos:prefLabel "P92 brought into existence"@en ;
                                                        skos:scopeNote """This property links an instance of E63 Beginning of Existence to the instance of E77 Persistent Item brought into existence by it.

It allows a “start” to be attached to any instance of E77 Persistent Item being documented, i.e., as instances of E70 Thing, E72 Legal Object, E39 Actor, E41 Appellation and E55 Type."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org//html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P93
<https://cidoc-crm.org//html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P93> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                        rdfs:domain <https://cidoc-crm.org//html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E64> ;
                                                        rdfs:range epont:Epigraphic_Support ,
                                                                   epont:Inscribed_Text ;
                                                        rdfs:comment "Inverse: was taken out of existence by"@en ;
                                                        skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                        skos:prefLabel "P93 took out of existence"@en ;
                                                        skos:scopeNote """This property links an instance of E64 End of Existence to the instance of E77 Persistent Item taken out of existence by it.

In the case of immaterial things, the instance of E64 End of Existence is considered to take place with the destruction of the last physical carrier.

This allows an “end” to be attached to any instance of E77 Persistent Item being documented i.e. instances of E70 Thing, E72 Legal Object, E39 Actor, E41 Appellation, and E55 Type. For many instances of E77 Persistent Item we know the maximum life-span and can infer that they must have ended to exist. We assume in that case an instance of E64 End of Existence, which may be as unnoticeable as forgetting the secret knowledge by the last representative of some indigenous nation."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmsci/html/CRMsci_v2.0.html#O19
<https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmsci/html/CRMsci_v2.0.html#O19> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                                    rdfs:domain <https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmsci/html/CRMsci_v2.0.html#S19> ;
                                                                    rdfs:range epont:Epigraphic_Support ,
                                                                               epont:Inscribed_Text ;
                                                                    rdfs:comment "Inverse: was object encountered through"@en ;
                                                                    skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                    skos:prefLabel "O19 encountered object"@en ;
                                                                    skos:scopeNote "This property associates an instance of S19 Encounter Event with an instance of E18 Physical Thing that was encountered or observed as present during the event."^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmsci/html/CRMsci_v2.0.html#O21
<https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmsci/html/CRMsci_v2.0.html#O21> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                                    rdfs:domain <https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmsci/html/CRMsci_v2.0.html#S19> ,
                                                                                <https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmsci/html/CRMsci_v2.0.html#S21> ,
                                                                                epont:Autopsy ;
                                                                    rdfs:range <https://cidoc-crm.org//html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E53> ;
                                                                    rdfs:comment "Inverse: witnessed encounter"@en ;
                                                                    skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                    skos:prefLabel "O21 encountered at"@en ;
                                                                    skos:scopeNote "This property associates an instance of S19 Encounter Event with an instance of E53 Place at which the things, which were encountered, were observed to be present. This may be given in absolute terms or in terms relative to the observed thing. The associated place must be within the boundaries of the E53 Place at which the S19 Encounter Event took place, if that has been given. Note, that the encountered object may be larger and extend beyond the place of encounter, such as a corner of a building being excavated."^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmsci/html/CRMsci_v2.0.html#O24
<https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmsci/html/CRMsci_v2.0.html#O24> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                                    rdfs:domain <https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmsci/html/CRMsci_v2.0.html#S21> ;
                                                                    rdfs:range epont:Epigraphic_Support ,
                                                                               epont:Inscribed_Text ;
                                                                    rdfs:comment "Inverse: was measured by"@en ;
                                                                    skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                    skos:prefLabel "O24 measured"@en ;
                                                                    skos:scopeNote "This property associates an instance of S21 Measurement with the instance of S15 Observable Entity to which it applied. An instance of S15 Observable Entity may be measured more than once. Material and immaterial things and processes may be measured, e.g. the number of words in a text, or the duration of an event."^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmsci/html/CRMsci_v2.0.html#O9
<https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmsci/html/CRMsci_v2.0.html#O9> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                                   rdfs:domain <https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmsci/html/CRMsci_v2.0.html#S21> ;
                                                                   rdfs:range <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E57> ;
                                                                   rdfs:comment "Inverse: property type was observed by"@en ;
                                                                   skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                   skos:prefLabel "O9 observed property type"@en ;
                                                                   skos:scopeNote """This property associates an instance of S4 Observation with the instance of S9 Property Type for which the observation provides a value or evidence, such as “concentration of nitrate” observed in the water from a particular borehole. Encoding the observed property by type, observed entity and value (properties O9, O10, O16) is a method to circumscribe the reification of the observed property by the respective instance of S4 Observation.

In an RDFS encoding, this circumscription can be transformed into an explicit representation of the observed property in terms of a formal ontology either by use of a reification construct or by the use of a Named Graph containing the observed property. The latter representation allows for more formal reasoning with the model, the former is more flexible about the kinds of observations."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/lrmoo/html/LRMoo_v1.1.1.html#R17
<https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/lrmoo/html/LRMoo_v1.1.1.html#R17> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                                    rdfs:domain <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX2_Writing> ,
                                                                                epont:Editing ,
                                                                                epont:Transcribing ,
                                                                                epont:Translating ;
                                                                    rdfs:range <https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/lrmoo/html/LRMoo_v1.1.1.html#F2> ,
                                                                               epont:Apograph ,
                                                                               epont:Edition ,
                                                                               epont:Inscription ,
                                                                               epont:Translation ;
                                                                    rdfs:comment "Inverse: was created by"@en ;
                                                                    skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                    skos:prefLabel "R17 created"@en ;
                                                                    skos:scopeNote "This property associates an instance of F2 Expression that was externalised during a particular instance of F28 Expression Creation event with that particular creation event. An instance of expression creation creates an instance of expression and also creates any expressions that are parts of that expression." .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/lrmoo/html/LRMoo_v1.1.1.html#R4
<https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/lrmoo/html/LRMoo_v1.1.1.html#R4> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                                   rdfs:domain <https://cidoc-crm.org//extensions/lrmooF3_Manifestation> ;
                                                                   rdfs:range <https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/lrmoo/html/LRMoo_v1.1.1.html#F2> ,
                                                                              epont:Apograph ,
                                                                              epont:Edition ,
                                                                              epont:Inscription ,
                                                                              epont:Translation ;
                                                                   rdfs:comment "Inverse: is embodied in"@en ;
                                                                   skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                   skos:prefLabel "R4 embodies"@en ;
                                                                   skos:scopeNote "This property associates an instance of F3 Manifestation with one or more instances of F2 Expression which are rendered by this instance of F3 Manifestation. The manifestation formats the expression(s) in the way they are to be presented to some public, including specifying the intended sensory impression (such as visual appearance or audio rendition)." .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/lrmoo/html/LRMoo_v1.1.1.html#R7
<https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/lrmoo/html/LRMoo_v1.1.1.html#R7> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                                   rdfs:domain epont:Inscribed_Text ,
                                                                               epont:Transcription ;
                                                                   rdfs:range <https://cidoc-crm.org//extensions/lrmooF3_Manifestation> ;
                                                                   skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                   skos:prefLabel "R7 exemplifies"@en ;
                                                                   skos:scopeNote """This property associates an instance of F3 Manifestation with an instance of F5 Item that is one of its exemplars or its only exemplar.
Instances of F5 Item correspond to the kinds of physical unit(s) specified in the manifestation, regardless of possible later changes.

Even though an item may exhibit defects with respect to the intended manifestation, it is still regarded to carry the manifestation, as long as it is produced or made accessible as a functional item by its creators."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/lrmoo/html/LRMoo_v1.1.1.html#R76
<https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/lrmoo/html/LRMoo_v1.1.1.html#R76> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                                    rdfs:domain <https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/lrmoo/html/LRMoo_v1.1.1.html#F2> ,
                                                                                epont:Apograph ,
                                                                                epont:Edition ,
                                                                                epont:Transcription ,
                                                                                epont:Translation ;
                                                                    rdfs:range epont:Edition ,
                                                                               epont:Inscription ,
                                                                               epont:Transcription ;
                                                                    rdfs:comment "Inverse: has derivative"@en ;
                                                                    skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                    skos:prefLabel "R76 is derivative of"@en ;
                                                                    skos:scopeNote """This property associates an instance of F2 Expression with another instance of F2 Expression which was its source or one of its sources. This property is not transitive. It is asymmetric and irreflexive.
This property is also a shortcut of the fully developed path: F2 Expression (1). P16i was used for: F28 Expression Creation. R17 created: F2 Expression (2).""" .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P128
<https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P128> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                        rdfs:domain epont:Inscribed_Text ,
                                                                    <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/html/CRMtex_v2.0.html#TX1> ;
                                                        rdfs:range <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E34> ,
                                                                   epont:Inscription ;
                                                        rdfs:comment "Inverse: is carried by"@en ;
                                                        skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                        skos:prefLabel "P128 Carries"@en ;
                                                        skos:scopeNote "This property identifies an instance E90 Symbolic Object carried by an instance of E18 Physical Thing. Since an instance of E90 Symbolic Object is defined as an immaterial idealization over potentially multiple carriers, any individual realization on a particular physical carrier may be defective, due to deterioration or shortcomings in the process of creating the realization compared to the intended ideal. As long as such defects do not substantially affect the complete recognition of the respective symbolic object, it is still regarded as carrying an instance of this E90 Symbolic Object. If these defects are of scholarly interest, the particular realization can be modelled as an instance of E25 Human-Made Feature. Note, that any instance of E90 Symbolic Object incorporated (P165) in the carried symbolic object is also carried by the same instance of E18 Physical Thing."^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P140
<https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P140> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                        rdfs:domain <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E15> ;
                                                        rdfs:range epont:Edition ,
                                                                   epont:Epigraphic_Support ,
                                                                   epont:Inscribed_Text ;
                                                        rdfs:comment "Inverse: was attributed by"@en ;
                                                        skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                        skos:prefLabel "P140 assigned attribute to"@en ;
                                                        skos:scopeNote """This property associates an instance of E13 Attribute Assignment with the instance of E1 CRM Entity about which it made an attribution. The instance of E1 CRM Entity plays the role of the domain of the attribution.

The kind of attribution made should be documented using P177 assigned property of type (is type of property assigned)."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P141
<https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P141> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                        rdfs:domain <http://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.3/E13_Attribute_Assignment> ;
                                                        rdfs:comment "Inverse: was assigned by" ;
                                                        skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                        skos:prefLabel "P141 assigned"@en ;
                                                        skos:scopeNote """This property associates an instance of E13 Attribute Assignment with the instance of E1 CRM Entity used in the attribution. The instance of E1 CRM Entity here plays the role of the range of the attribution.

The kind of attribution made should be documented using P177 assigned property of type (is type of property assigned)."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P190
<https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P190> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                        rdfs:domain epont:Edition ,
                                                                    epont:Inscription ;
                                                        rdfs:range <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E62> ;
                                                        skos:definition "" ;
                                                        skos:prefLabel "P190 has symbolic content"@en ;
                                                        skos:scopeNote """This property associates an instance of E90 Symbolic Object with a complete, identifying representation of its content in the form of an instance of E62 String.

This property only applies to instances of E90 Symbolic Object that can be represented completely in this form. The representation may be more specific than the symbolic level defining the identity condition of the represented. This depends on the type of the symbolic object represented. For instance, if a name has type “Modern Greek character sequence”, it may be represented in a loss-free Latin transcription, meaning however the sequence of Greek letters.

As another example, if the represented object has type “English words sequence”, American English or British English spelling variants may be chosen to represent the English word “colour” without defining a different symbolic object. If a name has type “European traditional name”, no particular string may define its content."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P2
<https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P2> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                      rdfs:domain crm:E24_Physical_Human-Made_Thing ,
                                                                  <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX10_Style> ,
                                                                  <https://cidoc-crm.org//html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E53> ,
                                                                  <https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmsci/html/CRMsci_v2.0.html#S19> ,
                                                                  <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E15> ,
                                                                  <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E28> ,
                                                                  <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E3> ,
                                                                  <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E34> ,
                                                                  epont:Allograph ,
                                                                  epont:Edition ,
                                                                  epont:Epigraphic_Support ,
                                                                  epont:Graph ,
                                                                  epont:Idiograph ,
                                                                  epont:Inscribed_Text ,
                                                                  epont:Inscription ,
                                                                  <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E54> ;
                                                      rdfs:range <https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/type_of_inscription> ,
                                                                 epont:Current_Location_type ,
                                                                 epont:Edition_Type ,
                                                                 epont:Identifier_type ,
                                                                 epont:Inscription_preservation_state ,
                                                                 epont:Observation_Type ,
                                                                 epont:Reproduction_Type ,
                                                                 epont:letter_form ,
                                                                 epont:script_type ,
                                                                 <https://qudt.org/3.2.1/vocab/quantitykind> ,
                                                                 <https://www.eagle-network.eu/voc/statepreserv.html> ,
                                                                 <https://www.getty.edu/research/tools/vocabularies/aat/> ,
                                                                 <https://www.getty.edu/research/tools/vocabularies/aat/SupportType> ;
                                                      rdfs:comment "Inverse: is type of"@en ;
                                                      skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                      skos:prefLabel "P2 has type"@en ;
                                                      skos:scopeNote """This property allows sub-typing of CIDOC CRM entities –a form of specialisation – through the use of a terminological hierarchy, or thesaurus.

The CIDOC CRM is intended to focus on the high-level entities and relationships needed to describe data structures. Consequently, it does not specialise entities any further than is required for this immediate purpose. However, entities in the isA hierarchy of the CIDOC CRM may by specialised into any number of sub-entities, which can be defined in the E55 Type hierarchy. E41 Appellation, for example, may be specialised into “e-mail address”, “telephone number”, “post office box”, “URL”, etc., none of which figures explicitly in the CIDOC CRM class hierarchy. A comprehensive explanation about refining CIDOC CRM concepts by E55 Type is given in the section “About Types” in the section on “Specific Modelling Constructs” of this document.

This property is a shortcut for the path from E1 CRM Entity through P41i was classified by, E17 Type Assignment, P42 assigned to E55 Type."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P34
<https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P34> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                       rdfs:domain <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E14> ;
                                                       rdfs:range epont:Epigraphic_Support ,
                                                                  epont:Inscribed_Text ;
                                                       rdfs:comment "Inverse: was assessed by"@en ;
                                                       skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                       skos:prefLabel "P34 concerned"@en ;
                                                       skos:scopeNote """This property identifies the instance of E18 Physical Thing that was assessed during an instance of E14 Condition Assessment.

Conditions may be assessed either by direct observation or using recorded evidence. In the latter case the instance of E18 Physical Thing does not need to be present or extant at the time of assessment."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P35
<https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P35> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                       rdfs:subPropertyOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P141> ;
                                                       rdfs:domain <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E14> ;
                                                       rdfs:range <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E3> ;
                                                       rdfs:comment "Inverse: was identified by"@en ;
                                                       skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                       skos:prefLabel "P35 has identified"@en ;
                                                       skos:scopeNote "This property identifies the instance of E3 Condition State that was observed in an instance of E14 Condition Assessment activity."^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P37
<https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P37> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                       rdfs:domain <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E15> ;
                                                       rdfs:range <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E42> ;
                                                       rdfs:comment "Inverse: was assigned by"@en ;
                                                       skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                       skos:prefLabel "P37 assigned"@en ;
                                                       skos:scopeNote """This property records the identifier that was assigned to an item in an instance of E15 Identifier Assignment.

The same identifier may be assigned on more than one occasion.

An identifier might be created prior to an assignment."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P46
<https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P46> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                       rdfs:domain epont:Epigraphic_Support ;
                                                       rdfs:range <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E25> ;
                                                       rdfs:comment "Inverse: forms part of"@en ;
                                                       skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                       skos:prefLabel "P46 is composed of"@en ;
                                                       skos:scopeNote """This property associates an instance of E18 Physical Thing with another instance of Physical Thing that forms part of it. The spatial extent of the composing part is included in the spatial extent of the whole.

Component elements, since they are themselves instances of E18 Physical Thing, may be further analysed into sub-components, thereby creating a hierarchy of part decomposition. An instance of E18 Physical Thing may be shared between multiple wholes, for example two buildings may share a common wall. This property does not specify when and for how long a component element resided in the respective whole. If a component is not part of a whole from the beginning of existence or until the end of existence of the whole, the classes E79 Part Addition and E90 Part Removal can be used to document when a component became part of a particular whole and/or when it stopped being a part of it. For the time-span of being part of the respective whole, the component is completely contained in the place the whole occupies.

This property is intended to describe specific components that are individually documented, rather than general aspects. Overall descriptions of the structure of an instance of E18 Physical Thing are captured by the P3 has note property.

The instances of E57 Material of which an instance of E18 Physical Thing is composed should be documented using P45 consists of (is incorporated in).

This property is transitive and asymmetric."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P62
<https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P62> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                       rdfs:domain epont:Epigraphic_Support ,
                                                                   <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E25> ;
                                                       rdfs:range <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E28> ;
                                                       rdfs:comment "Inverse: is depicted by"@en ;
                                                       skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                       skos:prefLabel "P62 depicts"@en .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P65
<https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P65> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                       rdfs:domain epont:Graph ,
                                                                   epont:Inscribed_Text ,
                                                                   <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/html/CRMtex_v2.0.html#TX1> ;
                                                       rdfs:range <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E34> ,
                                                                  epont:Idiograph ,
                                                                  epont:Inscription ;
                                                       rdfs:comment "Inverse: is shown by"@en ;
                                                       skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                       skos:prefLabel "P65 shows visual item"@en ;
                                                       skos:scopeNote """This property documents an instance of E36 Visual Item shown by an instance of E24 Physical Human-Made Thing.

This property is similar to P62 depicts (is depicted by) in that it associates an instance of E24 Physical Human-Made Thing with a visual representation. However, P65 shows visual item (is shown by) differs from the P62 depicts (is depicted by) property in that it makes no claims about what the instance of E36 Visual Item is deemed to represent. An instance of E36 Visual Item identifies a recognisable image or visual symbol, regardless of what this image may or may not represent.

For example, all recent British coins bear a portrait of Queen Elizabeth II, a fact that is correctly documented using P62 depicts (is depicted by). Different portraits have been used at different periods, however. P65 shows visual item (is shown by) can be used to refer to a particular portrait.

P65 shows visual item (is shown by) may also be used for Visual Items such as signs, marks and symbols, for example the 'Maltese Cross' or the 'copyright symbol’ that have no particular representational content.

This property is part of the fully developed path E24 Physical Human-Made Thing, P65 shows visual item, E36 Visual Item, P138 represents to E1 CRM Entity which is shortcut by, P62 depicts (is depicted by)."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://dcmi.github.io/bibo/#:locator
<https://dcmi.github.io/bibo/#:locator> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                        rdfs:domain <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX7_Written_Text_Segment> ;
                                        rdfs:range <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E62> ;
                                        rdfs:comment "\"A description (often numeric) that locates an item within a containing document or collection\"; takes a string value" ;
                                        skos:definition ""@en ;
                                        skos:prefLabel "locator"@en .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/carriesText
epont:carriesText rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                  rdfs:subPropertyOf <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P56> ;
                  owl:inverseOf epont:text_is_carried_on ;
                  rdfs:domain epont:Epigraphic_Support ;
                  rdfs:range epont:Inscribed_Text ;
                  rdfs:comment "Inverse: text is carried on"@en ;
                  skos:definition ""@en ;
                  skos:prefLabel "carriesText"@en ;
                  skos:scopeNote "This property specifically links an instance of an epont:Inscribed Text to the physical carrier (epont:Epigraphic Support) upon which it is found. This property is a short-cut, as a more detailed representation can, e.g., specify the location on the object of the Epigraphic Field (TX4 Writing Field) within which the text is located."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/features_are_also_found_on
epont:features_are_also_found_on rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                 skos:definition "" ;
                                 skos:prefLabel "P130i features are also found on"@en .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/genre_is_found_in
epont:genre_is_found_in rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                        rdfs:subPropertyOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P2> ;
                        owl:inverseOf epont:has_genre ;
                        skos:definition "" ;
                        skos:prefLabel "genre is found in"@en .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/has_date
epont:has_date rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
               owl:inverseOf epont:is_date_of ;
               rdfs:domain epont:Inscribed_Text ;
               rdfs:range <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E61> ;
               rdfs:comment "This is a short-cut for the more fully developed path epont:Inscribed text : TXP5i was written by : TX2 Writing : P2 has time-span : E52 Time-Span : P82 at some time within : E62 Time Primitive"^^xsd:string ;
               skos:prefLabel "has date"@en ;
               skos:scopeNote "This property is a short-cut intended to reflect the standard attribution to an inscription of a date (or more commonly a date range). Athough it is rarely made explicit, formally such a dating is a statement about the date of creation of the physical instance of the inscribed text, and so the act of TX2 Writing, and not, for instance, necessarily equivalent to a statement about the date of the creation of the object which bears the text, or a statement about the actual temporal extent of the persistence of the text or the object, each of which could be expressed independently. Such dates are normally expressed as a range, because precision is rarely possible (and, formally, even if narrowly dateable, the process of creation is not instantaneous)."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/has_edition
epont:has_edition rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                  owl:inverseOf epont:is_edition_of ;
                  rdfs:domain epont:Inscribed_Text ;
                  rdfs:range dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
                  rdfs:comment "Inverse: is edition of"@en ,
                               "This is a short-cut for the developed path epont:Inscribed Text : P128 carries epont:Inscription : R76i has derivative : epont:Edition : R4i is embodied in : F3 Manifestation : dcterms:Bibliographic Resource"^^xsd:string ;
                  skos:definition ""@en ;
                  skos:prefLabel "has edition"@en ;
                  skos:scopeNote """This property is intended to link an instance of a epont:Inscribed Text with a specific bibliographic reference to a published edition of that text.
This is a short-cut for the developed path which links the physical Inscribed Text that carries the underlying prototype Inscription (an F2 Expression) to the Edition, in the form of a publication and which is constituted as a new F2 Expression through the activity of epont:Editing."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/has_genre
epont:has_genre rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                rdfs:subPropertyOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P2> ;
                rdfs:domain <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E34> ,
                            epont:Inscribed_Text ,
                            epont:Inscription ;
                rdfs:range epont:Text_Genre ;
                rdfs:comment "Inverse: genre is found in"@en ;
                skos:definition ""@en ;
                skos:prefLabel "has genre"@en ;
                skos:scopeNote "This property associates an instance of an epont:Inscription with a formal classification according to a typology associated with literary genres (epont:Text Genre), and in particular the distinction between the use of prose or verse."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/has_inscription_type
epont:has_inscription_type rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                           rdfs:subPropertyOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P2> ;
                           owl:inverseOf epont:is_inscription_type_of ;
                           rdfs:domain epont:Inscribed_Text ;
                           rdfs:range <https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/type_of_inscription> ;
                           rdfs:comment "This property is a short-cut of the developed path: epont:Inscribed Text : P128 carries : epont:Inscription : P2 has type : epont:Inscription Type"^^xsd:string ;
                           skos:definition "" ;
                           skos:prefLabel "has inscription type"@en ;
                           skos:scopeNote "This property links a physical instance of epont:Inscribed Text to a classification of the inscription according to one of the domain-specific vocabularies, such as https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/type_of_inscription. This property is a short-cut of the developed path which goes via the abstract expression of the inscription, to reflect the usual practice of epigraphers."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/has_language
epont:has_language rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                   owl:inverseOf epont:is_language_used_in ;
                   rdfs:domain epont:Inscribed_Text ;
                   rdfs:range <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E56> ;
                   rdfs:comment "This is a short-cut for the developed path: epont:Inscribed Text : TXP5i was written by : TX2 Writing : TXP1 used writing system: TX3 Writing System : TXP6 encodes : E56 Language"^^xsd:string ;
                   skos:definition "" ;
                   skos:prefLabel "has language"@en ;
                   skos:scopeNote "This property associates an instance of a epont:Inscribed Text with an instance of an E56 language in which it is, at least partially, expressed. As such it is closely parallel to crm P72 has language, but reflects a developed path from the physical instance of the inscription (a TX1, subclass of E24) through the act of encoding language in written form, rather than a simple statement about the language of the abstract linguistic object. Following the EpiDoc guidelins, languages may be described using the IANA subtag registry at https://www.iana.org/assignments/language-subtag-registry/language-subtag-registry"^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/has_letter_form
epont:has_letter_form rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                      owl:inverseOf epont:is_letter_form_found_in ;
                      rdfs:domain epont:Inscribed_Text ;
                      rdfs:range epont:letter_form ;
                      rdfs:comment "This property is a short-cut for the developed path epont:Inscribed Text TXP4 has segment : TX7 Written Text Segment : TXP8 has component : epont:Graph : P65 shows visual item epont:Idiograph : P138 represents : epont:Allograph : P2 has type : epont:Letter Form"^^xsd:string ;
                      skos:definition "" ;
                      skos:prefLabel "has letter form"@en ;
                      skos:scopeNote "This property associates a physical instance of an inscribed text (epont:Inscribed Text) with the classification of an individual letter type present in that physical instance of the inscription, reflecting a common epigraphic statement such as 'Inscription A includes/employs/displays three-bar sigma'. Letter forms may be described by common terms such as 'three-bar sigma', or according to a standardised typology."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/has_linguistic_property
epont:has_linguistic_property rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                              rdfs:subPropertyOf <http://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.2/P165_incorporates> ;
                              owl:inverseOf epont:linguistic_property_is_found_in ;
                              rdfs:domain <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E34> ,
                                          epont:Inscribed_Text ,
                                          epont:Inscription ;
                              rdfs:range <https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/bilingualism> ;
                              rdfs:comment "Inverse: linguistic property is found in"@en ;
                              skos:definition ""@en ;
                              skos:prefLabel "has linguistic property"@en ;
                              skos:scopeNote "Inscriptions consist of text and or symbols (with some semantic meaning). These may reflect the use or influence of one or more languages, and the interactions between two or more languages are a feature of particular interest for socio-linguistic study. This property serves to capture the documentation of such features which may be found either in the text (as linguistic object) or in the physical form of the text (the Inscribed Text) consequent upon the choice of scripts and other symbols."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/has_script
epont:has_script rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                 rdfs:domain epont:Inscribed_Text ;
                 rdfs:range <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX13_Script> ;
                 rdfs:comment "This property is a short-cut of the developed path epont:Inscribed Text : TXP5i was written by : TX2 Writing : TXP1 used writing system: TX3 Writing System : TXP16 employs script : TX13 Script"^^xsd:string ;
                 skos:definition "" ;
                 skos:prefLabel "has script"@en ;
                 skos:scopeNote "This property associates a physical instance of an epont:Inscribed Text with the script that is employed (in full or in part) in the writing of the inscription to encode one (or more) languages, such as 'the Latin alphabet'. Following the EpiDoc guidelines, scripts may be described using the IANA subtag registry at https://www.iana.org/assignments/language-subtag-registry/language-subtag-registry."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/has_transcription
epont:has_transcription rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                        owl:inverseOf epont:is_transcription_of ;
                        rdfs:domain epont:Inscribed_Text ;
                        rdfs:range epont:Transcription ;
                        rdfs:comment "This property is a short-cut for the developed path: epont:Inscribed Text : TXP10i was deciphered by : epont:Autopsy : TXP15 recorded correspondence : TX12 Grapheme Sequence : TXP11i was transcribed by : epont:Transcribing : P108 has produced : epont: Transcription"^^xsd:string ;
                        skos:definition "" ;
                        skos:prefLabel "has transcription"@en ;
                        skos:scopeNote "This property associates a physical instance of an epont:Inscribed Text with the material item that is a (normally hand-written) transcription of that text, itself a new derivative Expression of the original Inscription, produced as an act of Expression Creation via the process of autopsy and the activity of transcribing."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/has_translation
epont:has_translation rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                      owl:inverseOf epont:is_translation_of ;
                      rdfs:domain epont:Inscribed_Text ;
                      rdfs:range dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
                      rdfs:comment "This property is a short-cut of the developed path epont:Inscribed Text : P128 carries : epont:Inscription : P73 has translation : epont:Translation : R4i is embodied in : F3 Manifestation : dcterms:Bibliographic Resource. More extended paths can also be similarly shortcut, such as epont:Inscribed Text : P128 carries : epont:Inscription : R76i has derivative : epont:Edition : P73 has translation : epont:Translation :  R4i is embodied in : F3 Manifestation : dcterms:Bibliographic Resource. The chain of derivative copies and editions between the original Inscribed Text and the Translation may be extensive, but will commonly not be formally documented or therefore capable of formal description."^^xsd:string ;
                      skos:definition "" ;
                      skos:prefLabel "has translation"@en ;
                      skos:scopeNote "This property is similar to crm P73 has translation, but in contrast to that property which simply links an instance of E33 Linguistic Object (A), to another instance of E33 Linguistic Object (B) which is the translation of A, the purpose of this short-cut property follows a longer and more developed path in order to directly link the original physical instance of an epont:Inscribed Text to a published translation. As in the case of P73 has translation, 'When an instance of E33 Linguistic Object is translated into a new language a new instance of E33 Linguistic Object is created, despite the translation being conceptually similar to the source.' Consequently, a new translation is also a new (and derivative) F2 Expression, and the act of epont:Translating is an act of F28 Expression Creation, which can be associated with an Actor and a source such as a published Edition (which is then its immediately prior source Expression). The publication of that translation is in turn an instance of an F3 Manifestation."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/is_date_of
epont:is_date_of rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                 rdfs:subPropertyOf owl:topObjectProperty ;
                 skos:definition "" ;
                 skos:prefLabel "is date of"@en .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/is_edition_of
epont:is_edition_of rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                    rdfs:subPropertyOf owl:topObjectProperty ;
                    skos:definition "" ;
                    skos:prefLabel "is edition of"@en .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/is_inscription_type_of
epont:is_inscription_type_of rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                             rdfs:subPropertyOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P2> ;
                             skos:definition "" ;
                             skos:prefLabel "is inscription type of"@en .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/is_language_used_in
epont:is_language_used_in rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                          rdfs:subPropertyOf owl:topObjectProperty ;
                          skos:definition "" ;
                          skos:prefLabel "is language used in"@en .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/is_letter_form_found_in
epont:is_letter_form_found_in rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                              rdfs:subPropertyOf owl:topObjectProperty ;
                              skos:definition "" ;
                              skos:prefLabel "is letter form found in"@en .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/is_transcription_of
epont:is_transcription_of rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                          rdfs:subPropertyOf owl:topObjectProperty ;
                          skos:definition "" ;
                          skos:prefLabel "is transcription of"@en .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/is_translation_of
epont:is_translation_of rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                        rdfs:subPropertyOf owl:topObjectProperty ;
                        skos:definition "" ;
                        skos:prefLabel "is translation of"@en .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/linguistic_property_is_found_in
epont:linguistic_property_is_found_in rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                      rdfs:subPropertyOf <http://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.2/P165_incorporates> ;
                                      skos:definition "" ;
                                      skos:prefLabel "linguistic property is found in"@en .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/text_is_carried_on
epont:text_is_carried_on rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                         rdfs:subPropertyOf <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P56> ;
                         skos:definition "" ;
                         skos:prefLabel "text is carried on"@en .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/transcribed
epont:transcribed rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                  rdfs:subPropertyOf crm:P12_occurred_in_the_presence_of ;
                  rdfs:domain epont:Transcribing ;
                  rdfs:range epont:Inscribed_Text ;
                  rdfs:comment "This is a short-cut for the more fully defined path which links transcription to autopsy to physical text as follows - epont:transcribing : TXP11 transcribed TX12 Grapheme Sequence : TXP15i was recorded by : epont:Autopsy : TXP10 deciphered text : epont:Inscribed Text."^^xsd:string ;
                  skos:definition "" ;
                  skos:prefLabel "transcribed"@en ;
                  skos:scopeNote "This property is a shortcut, intended to link the act of epont:transcribing directly to the physical object (an epont: Inscribed Text) which is the subject of the act of transcription, reflecting the specific and normal relationship understood by the act of transcription in the domain of epigraphy. As such it is a sub-property of P12 occurred in the presence of (which property describes the active or passive presence of an E77 Persistent Item in an instance of E5 Event without implying any specific role)"^^xsd:string .


###  https://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/html/CRMtex_v2.0.html#TXP2
<https://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/html/CRMtex_v2.0.html#TXP2> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                                         rdfs:domain <https://cidoc-crm.org//extensions/crmtex/html/CRMtex_v2.0.html#TX4> ;
                                                                         rdfs:range <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/html/CRMtex_v2.0.html#TX1> ;
                                                                         rdfs:comment "Inverse: is included within"@en ;
                                                                         skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                         skos:prefLabel "TXP2 includes"@en ;
                                                                         skos:scopeNote "This property describes the relation existing between a TX1 Written Text and the TX4 Writing Field, specifically created to accommodate the text, within which it is inscribed. This relation becomes quite relevant in the very frequent case where more than a single text is found on different areas of a specific support."^^xsd:string .


###  https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P14
<https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P14> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                           rdfs:domain crm:E12_Production ,
                                                                       crmtex:TX5_Text_Recognition ,
                                                                       <https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmsci/html/CRMsci_v2.0.html#S19> ,
                                                                       <https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmsci/html/CRMsci_v2.0.html#S21> ,
                                                                       <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E15> ,
                                                                       epont:Autopsy ,
                                                                       epont:Editing ,
                                                                       epont:Transcribing ,
                                                                       epont:Translating ;
                                                           rdfs:range <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E39> ;
                                                           rdfs:comment "Inverse: performed"@en ;
                                                           skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                           skos:prefLabel "P14 carried out by"@en ;
                                                           skos:scopeNote """This property describes the active participation of an instance of E39 Actor in an instance of E7 Activity.

It implies causal or legal responsibility. The P14.1 in the role of property of the property specifies the nature of an Actor’s participation."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P168
<https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P168> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                            rdfs:domain <https://cidoc-crm.org//html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E53> ;
                                                            rdfs:range epont:E94_Space_Primitive ;
                                                            rdfs:comment "Inverse: defines place"@en ;
                                                            skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                            skos:prefLabel "P168 place is defined by"@en ;
                                                            skos:scopeNote "This property associates an instance of E53 Place with an instance of E94 Space Primitive that defines it. Syntactic variants or use of different scripts may result in multiple instances of E94 Space Primitive defining exactly the same place. Transformations between different reference systems always result in new definitions of places approximating each other and not in alternative definitions."^^xsd:string .


###  https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P3
<https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P3> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                          rdfs:domain <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX7_Written_Text_Segment> ,
                                                                      <https://cidoc-crm.org//html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E53> ,
                                                                      epont:Edition ,
                                                                      epont:Inscription ;
                                                          rdfs:range <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E62> ;
                                                          skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                          skos:prefLabel "P3 has note"@en ;
                                                          skos:scopeNote """This property is a container for all informal descriptions about an object that have not been expressed in terms of CIDOC CRM constructs.

In particular, it captures the characterisation of the item itself, its internal structures, appearance, etc.

Like property P2 has type (is type of), this property is a consequence of the restricted focus of the CIDOC CRM. The aim is not to capture, in a structured form, everything that can be said about an item; indeed, the CIDOC CRM formalism is not regarded as sufficient to express everything that can be said. Good practice requires use of distinct note fields for different aspects of a characterisation. The P3.1 has type property of P3 has note allows differentiation of specific notes, e.g. “construction”, “decoration”, etc.

An item may have many notes, but a note is attached to a specific item.

[In the epigraphic domain P3, and its subproperty P190 has symbolic content, provide a simple mechanism for reporting the text of an Inscription, and potentially in several variant types, using P3.1 has type, such as diplomatic and interpreted; however it can also be used, e.g. to provide a reference to describe the extent/locaton of a text segment within an InscribedText]"""^^xsd:string .


###  https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P4
<https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P4> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                          rdfs:domain crm:E12_Production ,
                                                                      crmtex:TX5_Text_Recognition ,
                                                                      <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX2_Writing> ,
                                                                      <https://cidoc-crm.org//html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E63> ,
                                                                      <https://cidoc-crm.org//html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E64> ,
                                                                      <https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmsci/html/CRMsci_v2.0.html#S19> ,
                                                                      <https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmsci/html/CRMsci_v2.0.html#S21> ,
                                                                      epont:Autopsy ,
                                                                      epont:Editing ;
                                                          rdfs:range <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E52> ;
                                                          rdfs:comment "Inverse: is time-span of"@en ;
                                                          skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                          skos:prefLabel "P4 has time-span"@en ;
                                                          skos:scopeNote "This property associates an instance of E2 Temporal Entity with the instance of E52 Time-Span during which it was on-going. The associated instance of E52 Time-Span is understood as the real time-span during which the phenomena making up the temporal entity instance were active. More than one instance of E2 Temporal Entity may share a common instance of E52 Time-Span only if they come into being and end being due to identical declarations or events."^^xsd:string .


###  https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P43
<https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P43> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                           rdfs:domain <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX7_Written_Text_Segment> ,
                                                                       <https://cidoc-crm.org//extensions/crmtex/html/CRMtex_v2.0.html#TX4> ,
                                                                       epont:Epigraphic_Support ,
                                                                       epont:Graph ;
                                                           rdfs:range <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E54> ;
                                                           rdfs:comment "Inverse: is dimension of"@en ;
                                                           skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                           skos:prefLabel "P43 has dimension"@en ;
                                                           skos:scopeNote """This property records an instance of E54 Dimension of some instance of E70 Thing.

In the case that the recorded property is a result of a measurement of an instance of E18 Physical Thing, this property is a shortcut of the more fully developed path from E18 Physical Thing through P39i was measured by, E16 Measurement, P40 observed dimension to E54 Dimension.

It offers no information about how and when an E54 Dimension was established, nor by whom. Knowledge about an instance of E54 Dimension need not be the result of a measurement; it may be the result of evaluating data or other information, which should be documented as an instance of E13 Attribute Assignment.

An instance of E54 Dimension is specific to an instance of E70 Thing."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P45
<https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P45> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                           rdfs:domain epont:Epigraphic_Support ,
                                                                       epont:Inscribed_Text ;
                                                           rdfs:range <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E57> ;
                                                           rdfs:comment "Inverse: is incorporated in"@en ;
                                                           skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                           skos:prefLabel "P45 consists of"@en ;
                                                           skos:scopeNote """This property identifies the instances of E57 Materials of which an instance of E18 Physical Thing is composed.

All physical things consist of physical materials. P45 consists of (is incorporated in) allows the different materials to be recorded. P45 consists of (is incorporated in) refers here to observed material as opposed to the consumed raw material.

A material, such as a theoretical alloy, may not have any physical instances.

[In the epigraphic domain, note that formally one could describe the material composition of the object that has been engraved (the Epigraphic Support) or of the inscription itself on those occasions when it is composed of an inlaid or otherwise applied material that is distinct from the underlying physical object.]"""^^xsd:string .


###  https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P48
<https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P48> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                           rdfs:domain <https://cidoc-crm.org//html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E53> ,
                                                                       epont:Edition ,
                                                                       epont:Epigraphic_Support ,
                                                                       epont:Inscribed_Text ;
                                                           rdfs:range <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E42> ;
                                                           rdfs:comment "Inverse: is preferred identifier of"@en ;
                                                           skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                           skos:prefLabel "P48 has preferred identifier"@en .


###  https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P55
<https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P55> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                           rdfs:domain epont:Epigraphic_Support ;
                                                           rdfs:range <https://cidoc-crm.org//html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E53> ;
                                                           rdfs:comment "Inverse: currently holds"@en ;
                                                           skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                           skos:prefLabel "P55 has current location"@en ;
                                                           skos:scopeNote """This property records the location of an instance of E19 Physical Object at the time of validity of the record or database containing the statement that uses this property.

This property is a specialisation of P53 has former or current location (is former or current location of). It indicates that the instance of E53 Place associated with the instance of E19 Physical Object is the current location of the object. The property does not allow any indication of how long the object has been at the current location.

This property is a shortcut. A more detailed representation can make use of the fully developed (i.e., indirect) path from E19 Physical Object, through, P25i moved by, E9 Move, P26 moved to to E53 Place if and only if this Move is the most recent."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P56
<https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P56> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                           rdfs:domain epont:Epigraphic_Support ;
                                                           rdfs:range <https://cidoc-crm.org//extensions/crmtex/html/CRMtex_v2.0.html#TX4> ,
                                                                      <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/html/CRMtex_v2.0.html#TX1> ,
                                                                      <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E25> ;
                                                           rdfs:comment "Inverse: is found on"@en ;
                                                           skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                           skos:prefLabel "P56 bears feature"@en ;
                                                           skos:scopeNote """This property identifies something that is depicted by an instance of E24 Physical Human-Made Thing. Depicting is meant in the sense that an instance of E24 Physical Human-Made Thing intentionally shows, through its optical qualities or form, a representation of the entity depicted. Photographs are by default regarded as being intentional in this sense. Anything that is designed to change the properties of the depiction, such as an e-book reader, is specifically excluded. The property does not pertain to inscriptions or any other information encoding.

This property is a shortcut of the more fully developed path from E24 Physical Human-Made Thing through P65 shows visual item, E36 Visual Item, P138 represents to E1 CRM Entity. P62.1 mode of depiction allows the nature of the depiction to be refined."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P70
<https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P70> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                           rdfs:domain dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
                                                           rdfs:range epont:Epigraphic_Support ;
                                                           rdfs:comment "Inverse: is documented in"@en ;
                                                           skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                           skos:prefLabel "P70 documents"@en ;
                                                           skos:scopeNote """This property describes the CRM Entities documented as instances of E31 Document.

Documents may describe any conceivable entity, hence the link to the highest-level entity in the CIDOC CRM class hierarchy. This property is intended for cases where a reference is regarded as making a proposition about reality. This may be of a documentary character, in the scholarly or scientific sense, or a more general statement."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P82
<https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P82> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                           rdfs:domain <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E52> ;
                                                           rdfs:range <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E61> ;
                                                           skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                           skos:prefLabel "P82 at some time within"@en ;
                                                           skos:scopeNote "This property describes the maximum period of time within which an E52 Time-Span falls. Since Time-Spans may not have precisely known temporal extents, the CIDOC CRM supports statements about the minimum and maximum temporal extents of Time-Spans. This property allows a Time-Span’s maximum temporal extent (i.e. its outer boundary) to be assigned an E61 Time Primitive value. Time Primitives are treated by the CIDOC CRM as application or system specific date intervals, and are not further analysed. If different sources of evidence justify different maximum extents without contradicting each other, the resulting intersection of all these extents will be the best estimate. This should be taken into account for information integration."^^xsd:string .


###  https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P90
<https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P90> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                           rdfs:domain <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E54> ;
                                                           rdfs:range <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E60> ;
                                                           skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                           skos:prefLabel "P90 has value"@en ;
                                                           skos:scopeNote "This property allows an instance of E54 Dimension to be approximated by an instance of E60 Number primitive."^^xsd:string .


###  https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P91
<https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#P91> rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
                                                           rdfs:domain <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E54> ;
                                                           rdfs:range <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E58> ;
                                                           rdfs:comment "Inverse: is unit of"@en ;
                                                           skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                           skos:prefLabel "P91 has unit"@en ;
                                                           skos:scopeNote "This property shows the type of unit an instance of E54 Dimension was expressed in."^^xsd:string .


#################################################################
#    Classes
#################################################################

###  http://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.2/E37_Mark
<http://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.2/E37_Mark> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                rdfs:subClassOf <http://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.3/E36_Visual_Item> ;
                                                skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                skos:prefLabel "E37 Visual Mark"@en ;
                                                skos:scopeNote """This class comprises symbols, signs, signatures, or short texts applied to instances of E24 Physical Human-Made Thing by arbitrary techniques, often in order to indicate such things as creator, owner, dedications, purpose, or to communicate information generally. Instances of E37 Mark do not represent the actual image of a mark, but the abstract ideal (or archetype) as used for codification in reference documents forming cultural documentation.

This class specifically excludes features that have no semantic significance, such as scratches or tool marks. These should be documented as instances of E25 Human-Made Feature."""^^xsd:string .


###  http://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.2/E90_Symbolic_Object
<http://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.2/E90_Symbolic_Object> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                           skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                           skos:prefLabel "E90 Symbolic Object"@en .


###  http://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.3/E13_Attribute_Assignment
<http://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.3/E13_Attribute_Assignment> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                skos:prefLabel "E13 Attribute Assignment"@en ;
                                                                skos:scopeNote """This class comprises the actions of making assertions about one property of an object or any single relation between two items or concepts. The type of the property asserted to hold between two items or concepts can be described by the property P177 assigned property of type (is type of property assigned): E55 Type.

For example, the class describes the actions of people making propositions and statements during certain scientific/scholarly procedures, e.g. the person and date when a condition statement was made, an identifier was assigned, the museum object was measured, etc. Which kinds of such assignments and statements need to be documented explicitly in structures of a schema rather than free text, depends on whether this information should be accessible by structured queries.

This class allows for the documentation of how the respective assignment came about, and whose opinion it was. Note that all instances of properties described in a knowledge base are the opinion of someone. Per default, they are the opinion of the team maintaining the knowledge base. This fact must not individually be registered for all instances of properties provided by the maintaining team, because it would result in an endless recursion of whose opinion was the description of an opinion. Therefore, the use of instances of E13 Attribute Assignment marks the fact that the maintaining team is in general neutral to the validity of the respective assertion, but registers someone else’s opinion and how it came about.

All properties assigned in such an action can also be seen as directly relating the respective pair of items or concepts. Multiple use of instances of E13 Attribute Assignment may possibly lead to a collection of contradictory values."""^^xsd:string .


###  http://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.3/E17_Type_Assignment
<http://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.3/E17_Type_Assignment> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                           rdfs:subClassOf <http://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.3/E13_Attribute_Assignment> ;
                                                           skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                           skos:prefLabel "E17 Type Assignment"@en ;
                                                           skos:scopeNote """This class comprises the actions of classifying items of whatever kind. Such items include objects, specimens, people, actions, and concepts.

This class allows for the documentation of the context of classification acts in cases where the value of the classification depends on the personal opinion of the classifier, and the date that the classification was made. This class also encompasses the notion of “determination,” i.e. the systematic and molecular identification of a specimen in biology."""^^xsd:string .


###  http://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.3/E36_Visual_Item
<http://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.3/E36_Visual_Item> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                       skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                       skos:prefLabel "E36 Visual Item"@en ;
                                                       skos:scopeNote """This class comprises the intellectual or conceptual aspects of recognisable marks and images.

This class does not intend to describe the idiosyncratic characteristics of an individual physical embodiment of a visual item, but the underlying prototype. For example, a mark such as the ICOM logo is generally considered to be the same logo when used on any number of publications. The size, orientation, and colour may change, but the logo remains uniquely identifiable. The same is true of images that are reproduced many times. This means that visual items are independent of their physical support.

The E36 Visual Item class provides a means of identifying and linking together instances of E24 Physical Human-Made Thing that carry the same visual symbols, marks, or images, etc. The property P62 depicts (is depicted by) between E24 Physical Human-Made Thing and the depicted subjects (E1 CRM Entity) can be regarded as a shortcut of the more fully developed path from E24 Physical Human-Made Thing through P65 shows visual item (is shown by), E36 Visual Item, P138 represents (has representation) to E1 CRM Entity, which in addition captures the optical features of the depiction."""^^xsd:string .


###  http://purl.org/dc/terms/BibliographicResource
dcterms:BibliographicResource rdf:type owl:Class ;
                              skos:definition "A book, article, or other documentary resource."@en ;
                              skos:prefLabel "Bibliographical Resource"@en .


###  http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E12_Production
crm:E12_Production rdf:type owl:Class ;
                   skos:definition ""@en ;
                   skos:prefLabel "E12 Production"@en ;
                   skos:scopeNote """This class comprises activities that are designed to, and succeed in, creating one or more new items.

It specializes the notion of modification into production. The decision as to whether or not an object is regarded as new is context sensitive. Normally, items are considered “new” if there is no obvious overall similarity between them and the consumed items and material used in their production. In other cases, an item is considered “new” because it becomes relevant to documentation by a modification. For example, the scribbling of a name on a potsherd may make it a voting token. The original potsherd may not be worth documenting, in contrast to the inscribed one.

This entity can be collective: the printing of a thousand books, for example, would normally be considered a single event.

An event should also be documented using an instance of E81 Transformation if it results in the destruction of one or more objects and the simultaneous production of others using parts or material from the originals. In this case, the new items have separate identities and matter is preserved, but identity is not."""^^xsd:string .


###  http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E24_Physical_Human-Made_Thing
crm:E24_Physical_Human-Made_Thing rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                  skos:definition ""@en ;
                                  skos:prefLabel "E24 Physical Human-Made Thing"@en ;
                                  skos:scopeNote """This class comprises all persistent physical items of any size that are purposely created by human activity. This class comprises, besides others, human-made objects, such as a sword, and human-made features, such as rock art. For example, a “cup and ring” carving on bedrock is regarded as instance of E24 Physical Human-Made Thing.

Instances of E24 Physical Human-Made Thing may be the result of modifying pre-existing physical things, preserving larger parts or most of the original matter and structure, which poses the question if they are new or even human-made, the respective interventions of production made on such original material should be obvious and sufficient to regard that the product has a new, distinct identity and intended function and is human-made. Substantial continuity of the previous matter and structure in the new product can be documented by describing the production process also as an instance of E81 Transformation.

Whereas interventions of conservation and repair are not regarded to produce a new instance of E24 Physical Human-Made Thing, the results of preparation of natural history specimens that substantially change their natural or original state should be regarded as instances of E24 Physical Human-Made Things, including the uncovering of petrified biological features from a solid piece of stone. On the other side, scribbling a museum number on a natural object should not be regarded to make it human-made. This notwithstanding, parts, sections, segments, or features of an instance of E24 Physical Human-Made Thing may continue to be non-human-made and preserved during the production process, for example natural pearls used as a part of an eardrop."""^^xsd:string .


###  http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/TX12_Grapheme_Sequence
crmtex:TX12_Grapheme_Sequence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                              rdfs:subClassOf <http://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.2/E90_Symbolic_Object> ;
                              skos:definition ""@en ;
                              skos:prefLabel "TX12 Grapheme Sequence"@en ;
                              skos:scopeNote """This class comprises particular sequences of Graphemes used for representing the abstract written form of a section of a given particular text.
[i.e. a sequence of graphemes within a particular epont:Inscription, which can in turn be realised in the physical marks (graphs) on an epont:Inscribed Text]."""^^xsd:string .


###  http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/TX5_Text_Recognition
crmtex:TX5_Text_Recognition rdf:type owl:Class ;
                            skos:definition ""@en ;
                            skos:prefLabel "TX5 Text Recognition"@en ;
                            skos:scopeNote """This class comprises activities of recognizing physical features on some surface, often an instance of TX4 Writing Field, as an arrangement of a series of identifiable glyphs [graphs] of some known script, deciphered or not, in an order characteristic for a text.

For study purposes, the text recognition procedure requires a scientific autoptic examination of the text. An autoptic examination consists of an accurate analysis of the surface and the signs, and prescribes the use of specific tools and procedures for establishing the exact value of each sign on the physical feature. Deterioration of the original medium or “sloppy” writing may render parts of the original text as undecipherable or ambiguous, which may be annotated in the transcript following epigraphic standards; a text recognition typically results in a record of an equivalent sequence of graphemes on another persistent medium in a scholarly established form of representation of the respective graphemes, often called a “transcript”.

An instance of TX5 Text Recognition may in particular apply even to a single glyph, typically forming part of an instance of TX5 Text Recognition applying to a larger sequence of glyphs containing the former glyph.

The recognition process may be assisted by mechanical means, imaging technology, or a traditional squeeze for incised glyphs. In case the recognition process is solely based on the latter, the observation concerns only the representations on the latter as present to the researcher in some physical form or projection and should unambiguously be documented as such.

In case the recognized text has not been documented in a transcript, text recognition may constitute an implicit part of an overarching reading process, instance of TX14 Reading, which has resulted in other noteworthy propositions related to the content of the recognized text. On the other side, recognition of single glyphs or contracted parts of texts, as they are characteristic for the use of ligatured scripts, may quite well be implicitly supported by the reader’s comprehension of the text and the creator of the transcript may have chosen not to annotate parts that the reader regarded as unambiguous. Since these cases can often hardly be separated from the shape recognition of the glyphs in isolation, documenting such implicit comprehension as a separate process may not be relevant. It is however regarded as good practice to document explicitly the reading process and associated interpretative reasoning for any non-trivial resolution of ambiguity or gaps in the recognized text that has a bearing on the transcript or further completion of the transcript."""^^xsd:string .


###  http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/TX6_Transliteration
crmtex:TX6_Transliteration rdf:type owl:Class ;
                           skos:definition ""@en ;
                           skos:prefLabel "TX6 Transliteration"@en ;
                           skos:scopeNote """This class comprises activities of exactly re-writing (i.e., re-encoding) an instance of TX12 Grapheme Sequence, i.e., the characters of a text, a contiguous part or a single character of it, by using a writing system (TX3) different from that of the original text, without changing the order of characters or words, by using standard correspondences.

This operation may apply a 1:1 relation between the signs of the two writing systems, a “transliteration” in the narrower sense (e.g., the ALA-LC Romanization of Greek to Latin). It may also apply an approximation of the sounds of a language, as defined by the source writing system, by that of the target writing system, normally called a “transcription” (e.g., the “rōmaji” Romanization of Japanese), or a mixture of both (e.g. the ELOT 743 Type 2 – transcription of Greek to Latin letters). In a broader sense, the term “transcription” also applies to the activity of re-encoding a text using the same writing system (see example 1). The P16 used specific object (was used for) property can be used to specify the applied method of correspondence.

[Note: this scopenote in crmtex generates some confusion in its application of the words transcription and transliteration, since in English transliteration means specifically (OED) 'Replace (letters or characters of one language) by those of another used to represent the same sounds; write (a word etc.) in the closest corresponding characters of another alphabet or language'. In fact in English use 'transcribe', 'make a copy of in writing' can be the broader term, including transliteration. Since, however, the crmtex scopenote combines both activities under the term 'transliteration', we choose to make the specific act of 'transcription' as undertaken by epigraphers, that is, make a copy, normally a 1:1 copy in the same alphabet,  a sub-class of this crmtex class.]"""^^xsd:string .


###  http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX10_Style
<http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX10_Style> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                     skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                     skos:prefLabel "TX10 Style"@en ;
                                                                     skos:scopeNote "This class describes stylistic variations of texts, including local script styles (as the Carolingian minuscule for the Latin script) and individual scribal hands. [note the statement in crmtex that this corresponds to fonts and font variation, and that this should be considered a sub-class of E29 Design or Procedure should be considered problematic and requires revision]"^^xsd:string .


###  http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX13_Script
<http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX13_Script> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                      skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                      skos:note "[in crmtex this is formally a subclass of E89 Propositional Object, while ontograph/grapheme is an E90 Symbolic object in archetype / epont, but only an E55 type in crmtex; that both should be E90 seems more plausible and requires discussion, since this would in turn facilitate their linkage simply through P106 is composed of; whereas the current linkage via a subproperty of P67 refers to does not seem appropriate]"^^xsd:string ;
                                                                      skos:prefLabel "TX13 Script"@en ;
                                                                      skos:scopeNote "This class comprises functionally complete sets of mutually different graphemes [or ontographs] employed by one or more languages, regardless of the specific operating rules in a particular language. A TX3 writing system, on the other hand, also refers to the set of relations between symbols and linguistic units they represent. The same E56 language may be written using different scripts."^^xsd:string .


###  http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX2_Writing
<http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX2_Writing> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                      rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org//extensions/lrmooF28_Expression_Creation> ,
                                                                                      <https://cidoc-crm.org//extensions/lrmooF32_Item_Production_Event> ;
                                                                      skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                      skos:prefLabel "TX2 Writing"@en ;
                                                                      skos:scopeNote """This class describes the activity of communicating information by means of permanent, visible marks in a non-mechanical way, using various techniques (painting, sculpture, etc.) and by means of specific tools, on a given support.

As a subclass of F28 Expression Creation, note that 'Although F2 Expression is an abstract entity, a conceptual object, the creation of an expression inevitably also affects the physical world: when you scribble the first draft of a poem on a sheet of paper [or carve an inscription on a piece of stone] you produce an instance of F3 Manifestation and an instance of F5 Item [and so an epont:Inscribed Text]. F28 Expression Creation is a subclass of E12 Production because the recording of the expression causes a physical modification of the E18 Physical Thing that serves as the carrier [in this case the epont:Epigraphic Support].'

Consequently, the act of TX2 Writing is also, simultaneously, an act of F32 Item Production."""^^xsd:string .


###  http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX3_Writing_System
<http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX3_Writing_System> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                             skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                             skos:prefLabel "TX3 Writing System"@en ;
                                                                             skos:scopeNote "This class represents a conventional symbolic system designed to represent units of a natural language with the purpose of recording and transmitting information. A writing system implies both a set of symbols and a particular language, and employs a set of symbols (epont:Ontograph) to encode a particular E56 Language"^^xsd:string .


###  http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX7_Written_Text_Segment
<http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX7_Written_Text_Segment> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                                   rdfs:subClassOf epont:Inscribed_Text ;
                                                                                   skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                                   skos:prefLabel "TX7 Written Text Segment"@en ;
                                                                                   skos:scopeNote "This class describes portions of text considered to be of particular significance by scholars, as witnesses of a certain meaning or bearers of a particular phenomenon relevant to the investigation, study and understanding of a text. Examples of such text portions are columns, fragments, sections, paragraphs, as well as single words or signs, or other components of a written text. To each of these entities can be associated a single production event (TX2) or destruction event (E6), as in the case of letters or words damaged or worn by atmospheric agents or human interventions, as well as specific conditions (E3) for documenting its status during the text recognition process (TX5). The relationship between a written text (TX1) and its components is documented through the TXP4 has segment property."^^xsd:string .


###  http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX9_Glyph
<http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX9_Glyph> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                    rdfs:subClassOf <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX7_Written_Text_Segment> ;
                                                                    skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                    skos:prefLabel "TX9 Glyph"@en .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org//extensions/lrmooF28_Expression_Creation
<https://cidoc-crm.org//extensions/lrmooF28_Expression_Creation> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                 skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                 skos:prefLabel "F28 Expression Creation"@en ;
                                                                 skos:scopeNote """This class comprises activities that result in instances of F2 Expression coming into existence. An instance of F2 Expression is considered to be created when it is captured on a carrier other than the creator’s brain.
Although F2 Expression is an abstract entity, a conceptual object, the creation of an expression inevitably also affects the physical world: when you scribble the first draft of a poem on a sheet of paper, you produce an instance of F3 Manifestation and an instance of F5 Item. F28 Expression Creation is a subclass of E12 Production because the recording of the expression causes a physical modification of the E18 Physical Thing that serves as the carrier. The creation of an instance of F2 Expression coincides with the creation of the first instance of F3 Manifestation that R4 embodies (is embodied in) this instance of F2 Expression.

The P2 has type (is type of) property can be used to specify the type of the instance of F28 Expression Creation (i.e., activities such as translating, revising, or arranging music are types of creation process). The type of the process is distinct from the type of result even though the typology frequently used for instances of the resulting F2 Expressions may imply the category of the instance of the F28 Expression Creation.

An instance of F28 Expression Creation may use as source material one or more specific instances of F2 Expression. When the source expression is documented this is also expressed by the property R76 is derivative of (has derivative)."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org//extensions/lrmooF32_Item_Production_Event
<https://cidoc-crm.org//extensions/lrmooF32_Item_Production_Event> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                   skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                   skos:prefLabel "F32 Item Production"@en .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org//extensions/lrmooF3_Manifestation
<https://cidoc-crm.org//extensions/lrmooF3_Manifestation> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                          skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                          skos:prefLabel "F3 Manifestation"@en ;
                                                          skos:scopeNote """This class comprises products rendering one or more Expressions. A Manifestation is defined by both the overall content and the form of its presentation. The substance of F3 Manifestation is not only signs, but also the manner in which they are presented to be consumed by users, including the kind of media adopted.
An F3 Manifestation is the outcome of a publication process where one or more F2 Expressions are prepared for public dissemination, but it may also be a unique form created directly on some material carrier without the intent of being formally published.

An instance of F3 Manifestation typically incorporates one or more instances of F2 Expression representing a distinct logical content and all additional input by a publisher such as text layout and cover design. Additionally an F3 Manifestation can be identified by the physical features for the medium of distribution, if applicable. For example, publications in the form of hard-cover and paperback editions would be two distinct instances of F3 Manifestation, even though authorial and editorial content are otherwise identical in both publications.

In the case of industrial products such as printed books or music CDs, but also digital material, an instance of F3 Manifestation can be regarded as the prototype for all copies of it. In these cases, an instance of F3 Manifestation specifies all of the features or traits that instances of F5 Item display in order to be copies of a particular publication. In the case of industrial products, instances of F3 Manifestation are also instances of E99 Product Type, normally nowadays identified by characteristic identifiers such as ISBN numbers.

From the perspective of this epigraphic ontology, the F3 Manifestation has particular relevance to the production of an F2 Expression such as an Edition, in a publication."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org//extensions/crmtex/html/CRMtex_v2.0.html#TX4
<https://cidoc-crm.org//extensions/crmtex/html/CRMtex_v2.0.html#TX4> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                     rdfs:subClassOf <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E25> ;
                                                                     skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                     skos:prefLabel "TX4 Writing Field"@en ;
                                                                     skos:scopeNote "This class describes the portion of the physical carrier arranged and usually reserved and delimited for the purpose of accommodating a written text, highlighting and isolating it from the other parts of the object to which it belongs, enhancing and guaranteeing its readability. This entity is paramount specially in epigraphy, in which a specific element called “epigraphic field” has been defined by the discipline itself. Its importance is also evident in papyrology and codicology, where a clear distinction between area(s) containing the written text and empty parts of the support (margins, intercolumnia, etc.) is significant for the definition of styles and periods of the document."^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org//html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E53
<https://cidoc-crm.org//html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E53> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                        skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                        skos:prefLabel "E53 Place"@en ;
                                                        skos:scopeNote """This class comprises extents in the natural space where people live, in particular on the surface of the Earth, in the pure sense of physics: independent from temporal phenomena and matter. They may serve describing the physical location of things or phenomena or other areas of interest. Geometrically, instances of E53 Place constitute single contiguous areas or a finite aggregation of disjoint areas in space which are each individually contiguous. They may have fuzzy boundaries.

The instances of E53 Place are usually determined by reference to the position of “immobile” objects such as buildings, cities, mountains, rivers, or dedicated geodetic marks, but may also be determined by reference to mobile objects. A Place can be determined by combining a frame of reference and a location with respect to this frame.

It is sometimes argued that instances of E53 Place are best identified by global coordinates or absolute reference systems. However, relative references are often more relevant in the context of cultural documentation and tend to be more precise. In particular, people are often interested in position in relation to large, mobile objects, such as ships. For example, the Place at which Nelson died is known with reference to a large mobile object, i.e. H.M.S Victory. A resolution of this Place in terms of absolute coordinates would require knowledge of the movements of the vessel and the precise time of death, either of which may be revised, and the result would lack historical and cultural relevance.

Any instance of E18 Physical Thing can serve as a frame of reference for an instance of E53 Place. This may be documented using the property P157 is at rest relative to (provides reference space for)."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org//html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E63
<https://cidoc-crm.org//html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E63> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                        skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                        skos:prefLabel "E63 Beginning of Existence"@en ;
                                                        skos:scopeNote """This class comprises events that bring into existence any instance of E77 Persistent Item.

It may be used for temporal reasoning about things (intellectual products, physical items, groups of people, living beings) beginning to exist; it serves as a hook for both a terminus post quem and a terminus ante quem."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org//html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E64
<https://cidoc-crm.org//html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E64> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                        skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                        skos:prefLabel "E64 End of Existence"@en ;
                                                        skos:scopeNote """This class comprises events that end the existence of any instance of E77 Persistent Item.

It may be used for temporal reasoning about things (physical items, groups of people, living beings) ceasing to exist; it serves as a hook both a terminus post quem and a terminus ante quem. In cases where substance from an instance of E77 Persistent Item continues to exist in a new form, the process would be documented as instances of E81 Transformation."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmsci/html/CRMsci_v2.0.html#S19
<https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmsci/html/CRMsci_v2.0.html#S19> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                    skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                    skos:prefLabel "S19 Encounter Event"@en ;
                                                                    skos:scopeNote """This class comprises activities of S4 Observation (substance) where an E39 Actor encounters an instance of E18 Physical Thing of a kind relevant for the mission of the observation or regarded as potentially relevant for some community (identity). This observation produces knowledge about the existence of the respective thing at a particular place in or on surrounding matter. This knowledge may be new to the group of people the actor belongs to. In that case we would talk about a discovery. The observer may recognize or assign an individual identity of the thing encountered or regard only the type as noteworthy in the associated documentation or report.

In archaeology there is a particular interest if an object is found “in situ”, i.e. if its embedding in the surrounding matter supports the assumption that the object was not moved since the archaeologically relevant deposition event. The surrounding matter with the relative position of the object in it as well as the absolute position and time of the observation may be recorded in order to enable inferences about the history of the object.

In epigraphy, observations may range from the specific act of autopsy pertaining to text transcription (for which a designated sub-class of activity epont:Autopsy is defined) through to the many different acts of observation, identification and recording whicy may be undertaken of the inscribed text and its support in different times and places, ranging from original discovery to further study in a museum, etc."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmsci/html/CRMsci_v2.0.html#S21
<https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmsci/html/CRMsci_v2.0.html#S21> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                    skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                    skos:prefLabel "S21 Measurement"@en ;
                                                                    skos:scopeNote "This class comprises actions measuring instances of S15 Observable Entity, properties of physical things, or phenomena, states and interactions or events, that can be determined by a systematic procedure. Primary data from measurement devices are regarded to be results of an observation process."^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/lrmoo/html/LRMoo_v1.1.1.html#F2
<https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/lrmoo/html/LRMoo_v1.1.1.html#F2> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                   skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                   skos:prefLabel "F2 Expression"@en .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/lrmoo/html/LRMoo_v1.1.1.html#F5
<https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/lrmoo/html/LRMoo_v1.1.1.html#F5> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                   skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                   skos:prefLabel "F5 Item"@en .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E14
<https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E14> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                       skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                       skos:prefLabel "E14 Condition Assessment"@en ;
                                                       skos:scopeNote """This class describes the act of assessing the state of preservation of an object during a particular period.

The condition assessment may be carried out by inspection, measurement or through historical research. This class is used to document circumstances of the respective assessment that may be relevant to interpret its quality at a later stage, or to continue research on related documents."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E15
<https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E15> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                       rdfs:subClassOf owl:Thing ;
                                                       skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                       skos:prefLabel "E15 Identifier Assignment"@en ;
                                                       skos:scopeNote """This class comprises activities that result in the allocation of an identifier to an instance of E1 CRM Entity. Instances of E15 Identifier Assignment may include the creation of the identifier from multiple constituents, which themselves may be instances of E41 Appellation. The syntax and kinds of constituents to be used may be declared in a rule constituting an instance of E29 Design or Procedure.

Examples of such identifiers include Find Numbers, Inventory Numbers, uniform titles in the sense of librarianship and Digital Object Identifiers (DOI). Documenting the act of identifier assignment and deassignment is especially useful when objects change custody or the identification system of an organization is changed. In order to keep track of the identity of things in such cases, it is important to document by whom, when and for what purpose an identifier is assigned to an item.

The fact that an identifier is a preferred one for an organisation can be expressed by using the property E1 CRM Entity. P48 has preferred identifier (is preferred identifier of): E42 Identifier. It can better be expressed in a context independent form by assigning a suitable E55 Type, such as “preferred identifier assignment”, to the respective instance of E15 Identifier Assignment via the P2 has type property."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E28
<https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E28> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                       skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                       skos:prefLabel "E28 Conceptual Object"@en .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E3
<https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E3> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                      skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                      skos:prefLabel "E3 Condition State"@en ;
                                                      skos:scopeNote """This class comprises the states of objects characterised by a certain condition over a time-span.

An instance of this class describes the prevailing physical condition of any material object or feature during a specific instance of E52 Time-Span. In general, the time-span for which a certain condition can be asserted may be shorter than the real time-span, for which this condition held.

The nature of that condition can be described using P2 has type. For example, the instance of E3 Condition State “condition of the SS Great Britain between 22nd September 1846 and 27th August 1847” can be characterized as an instance “wrecked” of E55 Type. 

In the case of epigraphy, this might relate specificaly either to the preservation state of the inscribed text itself (epont:Inscribed Text) or of the physical carrier of the text (epont:Epigraphic Support), for each of which a controlled vocabulary may be used (epont:Inscription Preservation State; epont:Support Preservation State)."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E33
<https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E33> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                       skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                       skos:prefLabel "E33 Linguistic Object"@en .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E34
<https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E34> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                       rdfs:subClassOf <http://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.2/E37_Mark> ,
                                                                       <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E33> ;
                                                       skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                       skos:prefLabel "E34 Inscription"@en ;
                                                       skos:scopeNote """This class comprises recognisable texts that can be attached to instances of E24 Physical Human-Made Thing.

The transcription of the text can be documented in a note by P3 has note: E62 String. The alphabet used can be documented by P2 has type: E55 Type. This class is not intended to describe the idiosyncratic characteristics of an individual physical embodiment of an inscription, but the underlying prototype. The physical embodiment is modelled in the CIDOC CRM as instances of E24 Physical Human-Made Thing.

The relationship of a physical copy of a book to the text it contains is modelled using E18 Physical Thing. P128 carries (is carried by): E33 Linguistic Object.

In epigraphy, note that a specific feature of E34 Inscription is that it is at once a sub-class of E33 Linguistic Object and E37 Visual Mark, the latter of which extends to all marks which are considered to have some semantic significance, and therefore is not restricted more narrowly to script-based texts."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E52
<https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E52> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                       skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                       skos:prefLabel "E52 Time-Span"@en ;
                                                       skos:scopeNote """This class comprises abstract temporal extents, in the sense of Galilean physics, having a beginning, an end, and a duration.

Instances of E52 Time-Span have no semantic connotations about phenomena happening within the temporal extent they represent. They do not convey any meaning other than a positioning on the “time-line” of chronology. The actual extent of an instance of E52 Time-Span can be approximated by properties of E52 Time-Span giving inner and outer bounds in the form of dates (instances of E61 Time Primitive). Comparing knowledge about time-spans is fundamental for chronological reasoning.

Some instances of E52 Time-Span may be defined as the actual, in principle observable, temporal extent of instances of E2 Temporal Entity via the property P4 has time-span (is time-span of): E52 Time-Span. They constitute phenomenal time-spans as defined in CRMgeo (Doerr & Hiebel 2013). Since our knowledge of history is imperfect and physical phenomena are fuzzy in nature, the extent of phenomenal time-spans can only be described in approximation. An extreme case of approximation, might, for example, define an instance of E52 Time-Span having unknown beginning, end and duration. It may, nevertheless, be associated with other descriptions by which people can infer knowledge about it, such as in relative chronologies.

Some instances of E52 may be defined precisely as representing a declaration of a temporal extent, as, for instance, done in a business contract. They constitute declarative time-spans as defined in CRMgeo (Doerr & Hiebel 2013) and can be described via the property E61 Time Primitive P170 defines time (time is defined by): E52 Time-Span.

When used as a common E52 Time-Span for two events, it will nevertheless describe them as being simultaneous, even if nothing else is known."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E55
<https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E55> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                       rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E28> ;
                                                       skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                       skos:prefLabel "E55 Type"@en ;
                                                       skos:scopeNote """This class comprises concepts denoted by terms from thesauri and controlled vocabularies used to characterize and classify instances of CIDOC CRM classes. Instances of E55 Type represent concepts, in contrast to instances of E41 Appellation which are used to name instances of CIDOC CRM classes.

E55 Type provides an interface to domain specific ontologies and thesauri. These can be represented in the CIDOC CRM as subclasses of E55 Type, forming hierarchies of terms, i.e. instances of E55 Type linked via P127 has broader term (has narrower term): E55 Type. Such hierarchies may be extended with additional propertie"""^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E56
<https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E56> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                       rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E55> ;
                                                       skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                       skos:prefLabel "E56 Language"@en ;
                                                       skos:scopeNote """This class is a specialization of E55 Type and comprises the natural languages in the sense of concepts.

This type is used categorically in the model without reference to instances of it, i.e., the Model does not foresee the description of instances of instances of E56 Language, e.g.: “instances of Mandarin Chinese”.

It is recommended that internationally or nationally agreed codes and terminology are used to denote instances of E56 Language, such as those defined in ISO 639-2:1998 and later versions.

In EpiDoc based epigraphic encoding, standard practice is to use the IANA Language subtag registry (see further https://epidoc.stoa.org/gl/latest/supp-language.html)"""^^xsd:string .


###  https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E61
<https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E61> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                       skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                       skos:prefLabel "E61 Time Primitive"@en ;
                                                       skos:scopeNote """This class comprises instances of E59 Primitive Value for time that should be implemented with appropriate validation, precision, and references to temporal coordinate systems to express time in some context relevant to cultural and scientific documentation.

Instantiating different instances of E61 Time Primitive relative to the same instance of E52 Time-Span allows for the expression of multiple opinions/approximations of the same phenomenon. When representing different opinions/approximations of the E52 Time-Span of some E2 Temporal Entity, multiple instances of E61 Time Primitive should be instantiated relative to one E52 Time-Span. Only one E52 Time-Span should be instantiated since there is only one real phenomenal time extent of any given temporal entity.

The instances of E61 Time Primitive are not considered as elements of the universe of discourse that the CIDOC CRM aims at defining and analysing. Rather, they play the role of a symbolic interface between the scope of this model and the world of mathematical and computational manipulations and the symbolic objects they define and handle.

Therefore, they must not be represented in an implementation by a universal identifier associated with a content model of different identity. In a concrete application, it is recommended that the primitive value system from a chosen implementation platform and/or data definition language be used to substitute for this class."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/bilingualism
<https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/bilingualism> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                  rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E55> ;
                                                  skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                  skos:prefLabel "Bilingualism Feature"@en ;
                                                  skos:scopeNote "This specialised sub-class of E55 type comprises a hierarchical classification of inscriptions as linguistic objects which incorporate the concurrent use, or evidence of the presence of, two or more languages, for example Latin/Greek/'indigenous' languages, and which may therefore reflect societal and/or individual bilingualism/multilingualism. This is split into two main sub-categories, language-focused (and therefore pertaining to the content of the epont:Inscription as a subclass of E34 Inscription and so E33 Linguistic Object) and script-focused (and therefore pertaining to the form of the epont:Inscribed Text as a subclass of TX written Text and so E25 Human Made Feature), and the same epigraphic texts may contain multiple features, including from both main sub-categories. The recommended vocabulary is defined at https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/bilingualism"^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/execution_technique
<https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/execution_technique> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                         rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E55> ;
                                                         skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                         skos:prefLabel "Execution Technique"@en ;
                                                         skos:scopeNote "This sub-class of E55 Type provides a hierarchical classificatoin system that categorises inscriptions (epont:Inscribed Text) according to the technique and/or tools used to execute the text of the inscription (i.e. the act of TX2 Writing). The recommended typology is https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/execution_technique"^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/type_of_inscription
<https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/type_of_inscription> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                         rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E55> ;
                                                         skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                         skos:prefLabel "Inscription Type"@en ;
                                                         skos:scopeNote "The class is a sub-class of E55 Type with the specific aim of providing a hierarchical classification system that categorises inscriptions according to their primary societal function and content. The recommended vocabulary is that at https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/type_of_inscription"^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/Allograph
epont:Allograph rdf:type owl:Class ;
                rdfs:subClassOf epont:Character ;
                skos:closeMatch <http://archetype.ink/current/arch_Allograph> ;
                skos:definition ""@en ;
                skos:prefLabel "Allograph"@en ;
                skos:scopeNote "This class comprises different forms of an epont:Character according to a defined typology. This class does not intend to describe the idiosyncratic characteristics of an individual physical embodiment of an inscription, but the underlying prototype."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/Apograph
epont:Apograph rdf:type owl:Class ;
               rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/lrmoo/html/LRMoo_v1.1.1.html#F2> ,
                               <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E34> ;
               skos:prefLabel "Apograph"@en ;
               skos:scopeNote "This class comprises transcriptions of recognised instances of epont:Inscribed Text. This class is not intended to describe the idiosyncratic characteristics of an individual physical embodiment of transcription (e.g. the transcription written down in an epigrapher's fieldnotes), but the underlying prototype of that transcription."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/Autopsy
epont:Autopsy rdf:type owl:Class ;
              rdfs:subClassOf crmtex:TX5_Text_Recognition ,
                              <https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmsci/html/CRMsci_v2.0.html#S19> ;
              skos:definition ""@en ;
              skos:prefLabel "Autopsy"@en ;
              skos:scopeNote "Autopsy is the term reserved for the personal direct observation of the physical instance of an epont:Inscribed Text, usually, but not necessarily, resulting in the documentation of the inscription, including a transcription of the text, together with other information about the inscription and the physical carrier (epigraphic support) upon which it is found. Study of the epigraphic text by means of some intermediate reproduction (image, physical copy such as a squeeze, or a transcription or edition) does not formally constitute autopsy. As such autopsy is a sub-class not simply of S4 Observation in general (as is TX5 Text Recognition), but specifically of S19 Encounter Event (\"This class comprises activities of S4 Observation (substance) where an E39 Actor encounters an instance of E18 Physical Thing of a kind relevant for the mission of the observation or regarded as potentially relevant for some community (identity)\")."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/Character
epont:Character rdf:type owl:Class ;
                rdfs:subClassOf <http://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.2/E37_Mark> ;
                skos:closeMatch <http://archetype.ink/current/arch_Character> ;
                skos:definition ""@en ;
                skos:prefLabel "Character"@en ;
                skos:scopeNote "This class comprises different visual forms of a sign which fall between the level of generalised grapheme (also known as Ontograph in e.g. Archteype) and epont:Allograph. As such, it is arguably a subclass of Allograph, except that a Character cannot P138 represent an  Allograph but represents an arch Ontograph; it is therefore last in the chain of E37 Mark classes. In practice it is usually used to distinguish between majuscule and minuscule, where this applies, or between cursive and non-cursive forms. This class does not intend to describe the idiosyncratic characteristics of an individual physical embodiment of an inscription, but the underlying prototype. (following http://archetype.ink/current/arch_Character)"^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/Current_Location_type
epont:Current_Location_type rdf:type owl:Class ;
                            rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E55> ;
                            skos:definition ""@en ;
                            skos:prefLabel "Current Location type"@en ;
                            skos:scopeNote "This sub-class of E55 Type is intended to provide for the classification of repositories and other entities holding the physical objects that serve as epigraphic supports (e.g. museums, archaeological sites, libraries, etc.)."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/E94_Space_Primitive
epont:E94_Space_Primitive rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          skos:definition ""@en ;
                          skos:prefLabel "E94 Space Primitive"@en .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/Editing
epont:Editing rdf:type owl:Class ;
              rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org//extensions/lrmooF28_Expression_Creation> ;
              skos:definition ""@en ;
              skos:prefLabel "Editing"@en ;
              skos:scopeNote "This class comprises the activity of producing a curated and interpreted transcription, an epont:Edition, of an epont:Inscribed Text, normally according to established conventions (such as the Leiden Conventions) and normally for the purposes of publication. The activity of editing may rely upon many different sources in order to produce an Edition, such as the activity of Autopsy, a Transcription, other Editions, photographic or other reproductions of the original epont:Inscribed Text, etc. As the recreation and intepretation of the epont:Inscription, in a different format, the act of Editing is therefore an act of F28 Expression Creation, and creates a new (derivative) F2 Expression, in the form of an epont:Edition."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/Edition
epont:Edition rdf:type owl:Class ;
              rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/lrmoo/html/LRMoo_v1.1.1.html#F2> ,
                              <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E34> ;
              skos:definition ""@en ;
              skos:example "The published edition by T. Mommsen, in \"Inscriptiones Bruttiorum Lucaniae Campaniae Siciliae Sardiniae Latinae. Pars posterior. Inscriptiones Siciliae et Sardiniae\", Corpus Inscriptionum Latinarum, volume 10.2 (Berlin: G. Reimer, 1883), no.7190, of the inscription on a marble tablet that is Museo Archeologico Regionale Antonino Salinas, Palermo, inventory no.3501."^^xsd:string ;
              skos:prefLabel "Edition"@en ;
              skos:scopeNote "This class comprises the outcomes of the intellectual process of text editing by which an interpreted representation of an epont:Inscription is created, normally for publication. As such this constitutes an F2 Expression which includes written text (and potentiallly other marks or symbols), and so is also an instance of an E34 Inscription. As an F2 Expression it is necessarily derivative directly or indirectly of the canonical F2 Expression which is the original realisation of the epont:Inscription."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/Edition_Type
epont:Edition_Type rdf:type owl:Class ;
                   rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E55> ;
                   skos:definition ""@en ;
                   skos:prefLabel "Edition Type"@en ;
                   skos:scopeNote "This sub-class of E55 Type provides for the classification of epigraphic editions, traditionally based upon their greater or lesser proximity to original autopsy (compare, e.g. the CIL categories of 'descripsi', 'recognovi', 'contuli', etc.); consequently it might be inferred from explicit statements as to the source of the edition (autopsy, derivative of another edition, etc.), but might also be applied directly without more detailed explicit description of the process underlying creation of the edition."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/Epigraphic_Reproduction
epont:Epigraphic_Reproduction rdf:type owl:Class ;
                              rdfs:subClassOf crm:E24_Physical_Human-Made_Thing ;
                              skos:definition ""@en ;
                              skos:prefLabel "Epigraphic Reproduction"@en ;
                              skos:scopeNote "This class comprises physical human-made copies of an epont:Inscribed Text, either limited to the text alone (such as a paper squeeze) or reproducing some or all of the text-bearing object including the epigraphic field (such as a cast). Examples include squeezes, rubbings, casts, hand-made or manufactured physical copies. This class does not extend to photographic reproductions or scans, which are instances of E36 Visual Item. The type of epigraphic reproduction can be classified using P2 has type and epont:Reproduction Type"^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/Epigraphic_Support
epont:Epigraphic_Support rdf:type owl:Class ;
                         rdfs:subClassOf crm:E24_Physical_Human-Made_Thing ;
                         skos:definition "The physical carrier for an inscribed text, and so any human-modified or created object or feature capable of bearing a text."@en ;
                         skos:prefLabel "Epigraphic Support"@en .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/Graph
epont:Graph rdf:type owl:Class ;
            rdfs:subClassOf <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/versions/2.0/TX9_Glyph> ;
            skos:definition ""@en ;
            skos:prefLabel "Graph"@en ;
            skos:scopeNote "This class comprises individual marks made on a physical object and intended to represent signs (letters, punctuation, ligatures, etc.). This class is intended to describe the individual physical embodiment of the sign, not the underlying prototype (following the scopenote of http://archetype.ink/current/arch_Graph)."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/Identifier_type
epont:Identifier_type rdf:type owl:Class ;
                      rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E55> ;
                      skos:definition ""@en ;
                      skos:prefLabel "Identifier Type"@en ;
                      skos:scopeNote "This sub-class of E55 Type is intended to provide for the classification of different identifiers, such as museum inventory numbers."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/Idiograph
epont:Idiograph rdf:type owl:Class ;
                rdfs:subClassOf epont:Allograph ;
                skos:closeMatch <http://archetype.ink/current/arch_Idiograph> ;
                skos:definition ""@en ;
                skos:prefLabel "Idiograph"@en ;
                skos:scopeNote "This class comprises different forms of an epont:Allograph as produced by a given stone-mason or other creator of epigraphic texts. This class does not intend to describe the idiosyncratic characteristics of an individual physical embodiment of an inscription, but the underlying prototype (following http://archetype.ink/current/arch_Idiograph)."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/Inscribed_Text
epont:Inscribed_Text rdf:type owl:Class ;
                     rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/lrmoo/html/LRMoo_v1.1.1.html#F5> ,
                                     <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/html/CRMtex_v2.0.html#TX1> ;
                     skos:definition "The physical instance of an individual text inscribed or otherwise created on the physical surface of an object, that which is traditionally described as an 'inscription' by epigraphers (although this definition is broad and is not restricted to texts that are created solely through any one technique such as engraving)."@en ;
                     skos:example <https://sicily.classics.ox.ac.uk/inscription/ISic000001> ;
                     skos:prefLabel "Inscribed Text"@en .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/Inscription
epont:Inscription rdf:type owl:Class ;
                  rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/lrmoo/html/LRMoo_v1.1.1.html#F2> ,
                                  <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E34> ;
                  skos:definition ""@en ;
                  skos:prefLabel "Inscription"@en ;
                  skos:scopeNote "As per the parent class E34 Inscription, 'This class comprises recognisable texts that can be attached to instances of E24 Physical Human-Made Thing... This class is not intended to describe the idiosyncratic characteristics of an individual physical embodiment of an inscription, but the underlying prototype. The physical embodiment is modelled in the CIDOC CRM as instances of E24 Physical Human-Made Thing' (and specifically in epigraphy as an epont:InscribedText, subclass of a crmtex:TX1 Written Text). This epont class represents the inscribed text in the abstract. This class also constitutes a sub-class of F2 Expression, as the realisation of a Work which includes written text. Expressions are the outcome of the intellectual or creative process of realising a work. Subsequent Expressions conveying the same Work may be created over time. Although Expressions do not depend on a specific physical carrier and can exist on one or more carriers simultaneously (technically also possible in the epigraphic domain, where an inscription exists in multiple original physical instances), the epont:Inscription which is externalised onto a physical carrier as an epont:InscribedText is understood to constitute the canonical F2 Expression, as all subsequent representations of the inscription derive from it, either directly or indirectly. Since the form of F2 Expression is an inherent characteristic of the F2 Expression, differences in form imply different Expressions: consequently different forms of the original inscription resulting from, e.g.  the transcription, editing, modification, or translation. of the epont:Inscription constitute a new Expression."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/Inscription_preservation_state
epont:Inscription_preservation_state rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                     rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E55> ;
                                     skos:definition ""@en ;
                                     skos:prefLabel "Inscription Preservation State"@en ;
                                     skos:scopeNote "This sub-class of E55 Type provides for the classification of the preservation state or condition of the actual inscribed text (as opposed to the condition of the physical object which constitutes the epigraphic support / physical carrier)."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/Observation_Type
epont:Observation_Type rdf:type owl:Class ;
                       rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E55> ;
                       skos:definition ""@en ;
                       skos:prefLabel "Observation Type"@en ;
                       skos:scopeNote "This sub-class of E55 Type provides for the classification of acts of observation, according to the types of observation (encounter events) that may be relevant in recording information about an inscription or its physical carrier. Some suggested standard values are detailed in the EpiDoc guidelines at: https://epidoc.stoa.org/gl/latest/supp-histlocations.html. Alternatively, such a typology could be inferred from the explicit description of the observations undertaken."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/Ontograph
epont:Ontograph rdf:type owl:Class ;
                rdfs:subClassOf <http://cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/7.1.2/E90_Symbolic_Object> ;
                skos:closeMatch <http://archetype.ink/current/arch_Ontograph> ;
                skos:definition ""@en ;
                skos:prefLabel "Ontograph"@en ;
                skos:scopeNote "This class comprises the sign (letter, punctuation etc.) in its linguistic aspect. It is essentially equivalent to the grapheme but applies more generally (given also difficulties in defining grapheme). This class is intended to be independent of any physical or visual aspect of the sign. (following: http://archetype.ink/current/arch_Ontograph). Note that crmtex:TX8 Grapheme (https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/html/CRMtex_v2.0.html#TX8) is defined only as a subclass of E55 Type and should not be used here."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/Reproduction_Type
epont:Reproduction_Type rdf:type owl:Class ;
                        rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E55> ;
                        skos:definition ""@en ;
                        skos:prefLabel "Reproduction Type"@en ;
                        skos:scopeNote "This sub-class of E55 Type provides for the classification of different types of epigraphic reproduction, such as squeezes, rubbings, casts, etc."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/Text_Genre
epont:Text_Genre rdf:type owl:Class ;
                 rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E55> ;
                 skos:definition ""@en ;
                 skos:prefLabel "Text Genre"@en ;
                 skos:scopeNote "This class, as a sub-class of E55 Type, is intended to provide the ability to classify epigraphic texts according to text genre (as distinct from text type or function, such as 'funerary' or similar), and specifically at the top level to distinguish between prose and verse texts. A more detailed typology of verse metre could be employed here."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/Transcribing
epont:Transcribing rdf:type owl:Class ;
                   rdfs:subClassOf crmtex:TX6_Transliteration ,
                                   <https://cidoc-crm.org//extensions/lrmooF28_Expression_Creation> ,
                                   <https://cidoc-crm.org//extensions/lrmooF32_Item_Production_Event> ;
                   skos:definition ""@en ;
                   skos:prefLabel "Transcribing"@en ;
                   skos:scopeNote "This class refers to the specific activity by an observer of making a written copy of an epont:Inscribed Text, normally as part of the activity of Autopsy. The level of precision involved in the reproduction may vary considerably from a simple rewriting of the individual Graphs through to a precise and closely matching redrawing of the text. The act of transcribing normally reproduces the same writing system, making a more or less intepreted copy of the original inscription, with the implied intent of faithfully reproducing the original. The act of transcribing is therefore an act of Expression Creation, producing a new F2 Expression, an epont:Apograph, which is in turn realised in a unique (because handwritten) F5 Item, an epont:Transcription."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/Transcription
epont:Transcription rdf:type owl:Class ;
                    rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/lrmoo/html/LRMoo_v1.1.1.html#F5> ;
                    skos:definition ""@en ;
                    skos:prefLabel "Transcription"@en ;
                    skos:scopeNote "This class comprises the physical instances of individual transcriptions of epigraphic texts, realised through the act of transcribing. A transcription may in turn be part of a larger physical unit (so, e.g. a page in a notebook)."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/Translating
epont:Translating rdf:type owl:Class ;
                  rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org//extensions/lrmooF28_Expression_Creation> ;
                  skos:definition ""@en ;
                  skos:prefLabel "Translating"@en ;
                  skos:scopeNote "The act of translating the text of an epont:Inscription into another language, itself an act of Expression Creation, producing a new version of the epont:Inscription, whether directly from the original epont:Inscribed Text or via an intermediate Expression of the epont:Inscription such as an Edition, Transcription, or other Translation."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/Translation
epont:Translation rdf:type owl:Class ;
                  rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/extensions/lrmoo/html/LRMoo_v1.1.1.html#F2> ,
                                  <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E34> ;
                  skos:prefLabel "Translation"^^xsd:string ;
                  skos:scopeNote "The class comprises instances of translation of an individual epont:Inscription. The rendering of a text into a different language through the process of translation implies a different F2 Expression, and so the act of translating is also an act of Expression Creation. The translation is itself another instance of an E34 Inscription (a subclass of E33 Linguistic Object). The translation may be based upon the canonical F2 Expression, the original epont:Inscription and its epont:Inscribed Text, or it may be based upon an derivative Expression of the epont:Inscription, such as an epont:Edition."^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/letter_form
epont:letter_form rdf:type owl:Class ;
                  rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E55> ;
                  skos:definition ""@en ;
                  skos:prefLabel "Letter Form"@en ;
                  skos:scopeNote "a typology for classifying the variation of individual letterforms, at the level of idiograph or allograph"^^xsd:string .


###  https://ontology.inscriptiones.org/epont/script_type
epont:script_type rdf:type owl:Class ;
                  rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E55> ;
                  skos:definition ""@en ;
                  skos:prefLabel "Script Type"@en ;
                  skos:scopeNote "a typology for classifying individual combinations of letterforms, which collectively are considered to constitute a distinct style or type of a particular script, such as Latin monumental capitals."^^xsd:string .


###  https://qudt.org/3.2.1/vocab/quantitykind
<https://qudt.org/3.2.1/vocab/quantitykind> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                            rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E55> ;
                                            skos:definition ""@en ;
                                            skos:prefLabel "Dimension Type"@en ;
                                            skos:scopeNote "This sub-class of E55 Type is intended to provide for the clear description of the dimensions employed in the measurement of epigraphic texts and supports. The QuantityKind vocabulary provides one possible classification: https://qudt.org/3.2.1/vocab/quantitykind"^^xsd:string .


###  https://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/html/CRMtex_v2.0.html#TX1
<https://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmtex/html/CRMtex_v2.0.html#TX1> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                                        rdfs:subClassOf <https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E25> ;
                                                                        skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                                        skos:prefLabel "TX1 Written Text"@en ;
                                                                        skos:scopeNote "This class comprises visible or tactile marks (called glyphs or graphs), which relate in a systematic way to units of speech, intentionally traced (i.e., “written”) on some kind of physical support by using specific techniques and tools, with the purpose of conveying a message towards a given receiver or group of receivers."^^xsd:string .


###  https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E25
<https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E25> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                           rdfs:subClassOf crm:E24_Physical_Human-Made_Thing ;
                                                           skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                           skos:prefLabel "E25 Human-Made Feature"@en ;
                                                           skos:scopeNote "This class comprises physical features that are purposely created by human activity, such as scratches, artificial caves, artificial water channels, etc. In particular, it includes the information encoding features on mechanical or digital carriers."^^xsd:string .


###  https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E39
<https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E39> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                           skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                           skos:prefLabel "E39 Actor"@en ;
                                                           skos:scopeNote "This class comprises people, either individually or in groups, who have the potential to perform intentional actions of kinds for which someone may be held responsible."^^xsd:string .


###  https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E42
<https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E42> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                           skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                           skos:prefLabel "E42 Identifier"@en ;
                                                           skos:scopeNote """This class comprises strings or codes assigned to instances of E1 CRM Entity in order to identify them uniquely and permanently within the context of one or more organisations. Such codes are often known as inventory numbers, registration codes, etc. and are typically composed of alphanumeric sequences. Postal addresses, telephone numbers, URLs and e-mail addresses are characteristic examples of identifiers used by services transporting things between clients.

The class E42 Identifier is not normally used for machine-generated identifiers used for automated processing unless these are also used by human agents."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E54
<https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E54> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                           skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                           skos:prefLabel "E54 Dimension"@en ;
                                                           skos:scopeNote """This class comprises quantifiable properties that can be measured by some calibrated means and can be approximated by values, i.e. points or regions in a mathematical or conceptual space, such as natural or real numbers, RGB values, etc.

An instance of E54 Dimension represents the empirical or theoretically derived quantity, including the precision tolerances resulting from the particular method or calculation. The identity of an instance of E54 Dimension depends on the method of its determination because each method may produce different values even when determining comparable qualities. For instance, the wingspan of a bird alive or dead is a different dimension. Thermoluminescence dating and Rehydroxylation [RHX] dating are different dimensions of temporal distance from now, even if they aim at dating the same object. The method of determination should be expressed using the property P2 has type (is type of). Note that simple terms such as “diameter” or “length” are normally insufficient to unambiguously describe a respective dimension. In contrast, “maximum linear extent” may be sufficient.

The properties of the class E54 Dimension allow for expressing the numerical approximation of the values of instances of E54 Dimension adequate to the precision of the applied method of determination. If the respective quantity belongs to a non-discrete space according to the laws of physics, such as spatial distances, it is recommended to record them as approximations by intervals or regions of indeterminacy enclosing the assumed true values. For instance, a length of 5 cm may be recorded as 4.5-5.5 cm, according to the precision of the respective observation. Note, that comparability of values described in different units depends critically on the representation as value regions.

Numerical approximations in archaic instances of E58 Measurement Unit used in historical records should be preserved. Equivalents corresponding to current knowledge should be recorded as additional instances of E54 Dimension, as appropriate."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E57
<https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E57> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                           rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E55> ;
                                                           skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                           skos:prefLabel "E57 Material"@en ;
                                                           skos:scopeNote """This class is a specialization of E55 Type and comprises the concepts of materials.

Instances of E57 Material may denote properties of matter before its use, during its use, and as incorporated in an object, such as ultramarine powder, tempera paste, reinforced concrete. Discrete pieces of raw-materials kept in museums, such as bricks, sheets of fabric, pieces of metal, should be modelled individually in the same way as other objects. Discrete used or processed pieces, such as the stones from Nefer Titi's temple, should be modelled as parts (cf. P46 is composed of (forms part of): E18 Physical Thing).

This type is used categorically in the model without reference to instances of it, i.e. the Model does not foresee the description of instances of instances of E57 Material, e.g. “instances of gold”.

It is recommended that internationally or nationally agreed codes and terminology should be used: within the frame of epigraphy, this might be the EAGLE materials vocabulary, or the (forthcoming) FAIR Epigraphy materials vocabulary."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E58
<https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E58> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                           rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E55> ;
                                                           skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                           skos:prefLabel "E58 Measurement Unit"@en ;
                                                           skos:scopeNote """This class is a specialization of E55 Type and comprises the types of measurement units: feet, inches, centimetres, litres, lumens, etc.

This type is used categorically in the model without reference to instances of it, i.e. the model does not foresee the description of instances of instances of E58 Measurement Unit, e.g. “instances of cm”.

Système International (SI) units or internationally recognized non-SI terms should be used whenever possible, such as those defined by ISO80000:2009. Archaic Measurement Units used in historical records should be preserved."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E60
<https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E60> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                           skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                           skos:prefLabel "E60 Number"@en ;
                                                           skos:scopeNote """This class comprises any encoding of computable (algebraic) values such as integers, real numbers, complex numbers, vectors, tensors, etc., including intervals of these values to express limited precision.

Numbers are fundamentally distinct from identifiers in continua, which are instances of E41 Appellation, such as Gregorian dates or spatial coordinates, even though their encoding may be similar. Instances of E60 Number can be combined with each other in algebraic operations to yield other instances of E60 Number, e.g. 1 + 1 = 2. Identifiers in continua may be combined with numbers expressing distances to yield new identifiers, e.g., 1924-01-31 + 2 days = 1924-02-02. Cf. E54 Dimension."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E62
<https://www.cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E62> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                           skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                           skos:prefLabel "E62 String"@en ;
                                                           skos:scopeNote """This class comprises coherent sequences of binary-encoded symbols. They correspond to the content of an instance of E90 Symbolic object. Instances of E62 String represent only the symbol sequence itself. They may or may not contain a language code.

In contrast, instances of other subclasses of E59 Primitive value represent entities in mathematical spaces other than that of symbol sequences, by using binary-encoded symbols, such as date expressions or numbers in decimal encoding. For instance, different syntactic forms of a date expression may represent the same date but consist of different strings."""^^xsd:string .


###  https://www.eagle-network.eu/voc/statepreserv.html
<https://www.eagle-network.eu/voc/statepreserv.html> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                     rdfs:subClassOf <https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.3.html#E55> ;
                                                     skos:definition ""@en ;
                                                     skos:prefLabel "Support Preservation State"@en ;
                                                     skos:scopeNote "This sub-class of E55 Type is intended to provide for the classification of the surviving preservation state of the physical carrier for an epont:Inscribed Text. The current recommended vocabulary is https://www.eagle-network.eu/voc/statepreserv.html"^^xsd:string .


